WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.010 --> 00:00:07.095
&gt;&gt; Hey, welcome everyone.

00:00:07.096 --> 00:00:10.710
I'm delighted this
afternoon to introduce

00:00:10.711 --> 00:00:14.645
our AI distinguished
lecturer Cynthia Dwork,

00:00:14.646 --> 00:00:16.195
who many of you know.

00:00:16.196 --> 00:00:19.710
Cynthia is one of the most
accomplished technical women

00:00:19.711 --> 00:00:21.210
who I've ever been around.

00:00:21.211 --> 00:00:24.160
She has four fancy titles.

00:00:24.161 --> 00:00:26.495
Right now, she's
the Gordon MacKay Professor

00:00:26.496 --> 00:00:28.770
at Harvard in the Computer
Science department.

00:00:28.771 --> 00:00:31.530
She's also the Radcliffe
Alumna Professor

00:00:31.531 --> 00:00:34.130
at the Radcliffe Institute
for Advanced Studies,

00:00:34.131 --> 00:00:37.105
and she's an Affiliate Faculty
at the Harvard Law School.

00:00:37.106 --> 00:00:38.480
In addition to that, she's

00:00:38.481 --> 00:00:41.140
a distinguished Scientist
at Microsoft Research.

00:00:41.141 --> 00:00:43.940
She's was one of
the inaugural class of

00:00:43.941 --> 00:00:45.980
distinguished scientists
here about a decade

00:00:45.981 --> 00:00:47.935
or so ago, maybe
a little bit more.

00:00:47.936 --> 00:00:52.535
Cynthia received her PhD at
Cornell with John Hopcroft.

00:00:52.536 --> 00:00:54.500
From there she did a couple of

00:00:54.501 --> 00:00:57.070
year Postdoc with
Nancy Lynch at MIT,

00:00:57.071 --> 00:00:58.730
and then went on to
spend a fair amount of

00:00:58.731 --> 00:01:00.680
time at IBM Almaden,

00:01:00.681 --> 00:01:01.730
where she did a lot of

00:01:01.731 --> 00:01:03.710
foundational work that
I was not all that

00:01:03.711 --> 00:01:07.700
familiar with till earlier today.

00:01:07.701 --> 00:01:09.920
The more I knew about
that she did there

00:01:09.921 --> 00:01:11.740
was something on
rank aggregation,

00:01:11.741 --> 00:01:14.180
which was very important
in web search.

00:01:14.181 --> 00:01:18.495
She also did work on the
cryptography foundations,

00:01:18.496 --> 00:01:20.180
as well as proof of work,

00:01:20.181 --> 00:01:23.930
which is one of the underlying
foundations of Bitcoin.

00:01:23.931 --> 00:01:28.675
At MSR, she is well known for
two other strands of work.

00:01:28.676 --> 00:01:30.440
One is on differential privacy,

00:01:30.441 --> 00:01:33.610
putting privacy on
theoretical firm ground,

00:01:33.611 --> 00:01:35.830
and it's so incredibly
important today.

00:01:35.831 --> 00:01:39.275
What she'll talk about
with us this afternoon

00:01:39.276 --> 00:01:43.205
is more nascent work on
algorithmic fairness.

00:01:43.206 --> 00:01:46.310
This work, and her lifelong
accomplishments have

00:01:46.311 --> 00:01:49.500
been acknowledged by more prizes,

00:01:49.501 --> 00:01:50.610
and I name, more prizes,

00:01:50.611 --> 00:01:52.910
and the fancy titles I
mentioned at the outset.

00:01:52.911 --> 00:01:55.475
She's obviously
a fellow of the ACM.

00:01:55.476 --> 00:01:57.665
She won the Dijkstra Prize.

00:01:57.666 --> 00:01:59.090
More than a decade ago,

00:01:59.091 --> 00:02:00.785
she won the Go del prize.

00:02:00.786 --> 00:02:03.950
She's one of the very few
people who are members of

00:02:03.951 --> 00:02:08.440
both the National
Academy of Science,

00:02:08.441 --> 00:02:10.295
and National Academy
of Engineering.

00:02:10.296 --> 00:02:14.370
She's an amazingly
a depth scientist,

00:02:14.371 --> 00:02:15.860
and one who picks problems

00:02:15.861 --> 00:02:17.240
that have real practical impact,

00:02:17.241 --> 00:02:19.445
which I think is
a really unique skill.

00:02:19.446 --> 00:02:20.725
So, with that introduction,

00:02:20.726 --> 00:02:22.235
I'd like to introduce Cynthia,

00:02:22.236 --> 00:02:23.300
who will tell us about some of

00:02:23.301 --> 00:02:25.860
her work on algorithmic fairness.

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:35.340
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much for
that gracious introduction.

00:02:35.341 --> 00:02:37.245
Thank you for inviting me,

00:02:37.246 --> 00:02:42.865
and I'm really very
happy to be here.

00:02:42.866 --> 00:02:44.620
I have a couple of questions

00:02:44.621 --> 00:02:46.090
before I proceed with the talk.

00:02:46.091 --> 00:02:48.580
So, first of all,
how many people here are

00:02:48.581 --> 00:02:51.980
working in Machine
Learning and AI?

00:02:51.981 --> 00:02:54.610
Okay. How many people

00:02:54.611 --> 00:02:58.540
here have worked on
fairness particularly?

00:02:58.541 --> 00:03:03.620
All right, and any theoretical
computer scientists?

00:03:03.621 --> 00:03:06.800
Great. All right, good.

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:10.100
That helps me figure out
what I should be saying.

00:03:10.101 --> 00:03:16.145
This is a research effort

00:03:16.146 --> 00:03:19.365
that actually began at Microsoft,

00:03:19.366 --> 00:03:22.125
now defunct Silicon Valley Lab.

00:03:22.126 --> 00:03:26.000
Started in approximately 2010.

00:03:26.001 --> 00:03:29.480
I should mention that all
of the good slides in

00:03:29.481 --> 00:03:33.390
the talk are due to my student
Cristina Elemental.

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:36.295
Here's our problem.

00:03:36.296 --> 00:03:38.479
We're interested in
algorithmic fairness,

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:41.450
will be more specific in
a minute. We have a population.

00:03:41.451 --> 00:03:44.365
Our population is diverse
in many different ways,

00:03:44.366 --> 00:03:47.520
ethnic diversity,
religious diversity,

00:03:47.521 --> 00:03:51.275
geographic, medical, and so on.

00:03:51.276 --> 00:03:53.420
Well, whatever it
is we want to do,

00:03:53.421 --> 00:03:54.440
we want to be sure that

00:03:54.441 --> 00:03:56.880
somehow or other
we're being fair.

00:03:56.881 --> 00:03:59.870
Typical examples would
be something like this.

00:03:59.871 --> 00:04:02.300
We have a bank that receives

00:04:02.301 --> 00:04:06.020
detailed user
information about you,

00:04:06.021 --> 00:04:08.985
before giving you
your online experience.

00:04:08.986 --> 00:04:10.790
You surf on over to some bank,

00:04:10.791 --> 00:04:12.610
and before they
show you anything,

00:04:12.611 --> 00:04:14.370
they consult with
tracking networks,

00:04:14.371 --> 00:04:17.585
and find out stuff about you.

00:04:17.586 --> 00:04:21.560
The concern, and this was
a concern that was raised in

00:04:21.561 --> 00:04:25.970
a newspaper article in
the Wall Street Journal,

00:04:25.971 --> 00:04:28.125
in a series that they ran in

00:04:28.126 --> 00:04:31.250
2010 called, "What they know."

00:04:31.251 --> 00:04:33.200
It was mostly about privacy,

00:04:33.201 --> 00:04:36.305
but it also raised
the following fairness concern.

00:04:36.306 --> 00:04:41.030
The concern was that of
the illegal practice of

00:04:41.031 --> 00:04:43.100
steering minorities to credit

00:04:43.101 --> 00:04:45.795
card offerings of
less desirable terms.

00:04:45.796 --> 00:04:48.050
The financial industry
is more heavily

00:04:48.051 --> 00:04:51.590
regulated than
many other industries,

00:04:51.591 --> 00:04:54.930
and so this problem
really was a legal one,

00:04:54.931 --> 00:04:58.700
not just "a fairness one."

00:04:59.630 --> 00:05:04.375
Just to dispense with
some obvious things,

00:05:04.376 --> 00:05:07.925
one suggestion is
of course to hide

00:05:07.926 --> 00:05:11.240
the sensitive information
from the classifier.

00:05:11.241 --> 00:05:12.710
Whether you're
a member of a minority

00:05:12.711 --> 00:05:14.400
group or not would be hidden.

00:05:14.401 --> 00:05:17.620
That falls victim to
a couple of things.

00:05:17.621 --> 00:05:21.035
First of all, there's red lining.

00:05:21.036 --> 00:05:23.280
Who knows the term red lining?

00:05:23.281 --> 00:05:25.380
Great. We'll see a picture
of it in a minute,

00:05:25.381 --> 00:05:28.340
and on the web
there's web lining.

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:34.685
This is basically say
when zip code is used

00:05:34.686 --> 00:05:38.450
as a redundant encoding of race.

00:05:38.451 --> 00:05:40.850
It's illegal to
discriminate based on race,

00:05:40.851 --> 00:05:43.885
it's not illegal to
discriminate based on zip code.

00:05:43.886 --> 00:05:48.260
We have this issue now in
general of redundant encoding.

00:05:48.261 --> 00:05:50.240
Sensitive attributes
can be embedded

00:05:50.241 --> 00:05:53.600
holographically in
your other attributes.

00:05:53.601 --> 00:05:57.705
The other is a Birds of
a Feather phenomenon.

00:05:57.706 --> 00:06:00.920
Has anybody seen
this picture before?

00:06:00.921 --> 00:06:06.440
This was an undergraduate
research project

00:06:06.441 --> 00:06:07.780
at a class at MIT.

00:06:07.781 --> 00:06:10.950
This is not a
full-fledged studying,

00:06:10.951 --> 00:06:12.830
but essentially what the students

00:06:12.831 --> 00:06:14.735
found was the following.

00:06:14.736 --> 00:06:20.080
If you're male,
and something like

00:06:20.081 --> 00:06:23.490
five percent of your friends on

00:06:23.491 --> 00:06:27.815
Facebook are
self-described as gay.

00:06:27.816 --> 00:06:29.660
Then you're very
likely to be gay.

00:06:29.661 --> 00:06:31.580
You may not choose to

00:06:31.581 --> 00:06:33.920
release this information
about yourself,

00:06:33.921 --> 00:06:35.370
but from this Birds of

00:06:35.371 --> 00:06:37.535
a Feather flock
together phenomenon,

00:06:37.536 --> 00:06:41.070
it can be determined from
the attributes of your friends.

00:06:41.071 --> 00:06:45.020
Hiding the sensitive attribute
is a little dicing.

00:06:45.021 --> 00:06:50.630
Now, there's another problem

00:06:50.631 --> 00:06:53.435
with hiding the
sensitive information.

00:06:53.436 --> 00:06:56.520
That is that in some sense

00:06:57.400 --> 00:07:01.880
culturally aware
algorithms can be more

00:07:01.881 --> 00:07:06.420
accurate than algorithms
that survive.

00:07:06.421 --> 00:07:08.600
If you have a culturally
aware algorithm,

00:07:08.601 --> 00:07:10.310
it can make very good use of

00:07:10.311 --> 00:07:14.265
this sensitive information
to be more accurate.

00:07:14.266 --> 00:07:16.750
In a typical example,
and by the way,

00:07:16.751 --> 00:07:18.590
let me introduce some populations

00:07:18.591 --> 00:07:20.995
that I'm going to be
using in the talk.

00:07:20.996 --> 00:07:25.070
We all like food or
most of us like food.

00:07:25.071 --> 00:07:26.510
Some people like to have

00:07:26.511 --> 00:07:29.810
their food flavored
with the herb sage,

00:07:29.811 --> 00:07:32.090
and the rest prefer to have

00:07:32.091 --> 00:07:34.790
their food flavored
with the herb thyme.

00:07:34.791 --> 00:07:36.580
So, my groups are going to be

00:07:36.581 --> 00:07:39.700
the thyme eaters and
the sage eaters.

00:07:41.310 --> 00:07:44.920
Generally, sage will
be the minority group,

00:07:44.921 --> 00:07:48.680
but not everything
will require that.

00:07:48.681 --> 00:07:51.620
I'm going to be
calling them S and

00:07:51.621 --> 00:07:54.515
T. Maybe you have a situation

00:07:54.516 --> 00:07:56.330
where hearing voices is

00:07:56.331 --> 00:08:00.575
a very common religious
experience among the sage eaters,

00:08:00.576 --> 00:08:03.035
but among the thyme eaters it's

00:08:03.036 --> 00:08:05.915
a diagnostic criterion
for schizophrenia.

00:08:05.916 --> 00:08:08.380
This is a real medical example.

00:08:08.381 --> 00:08:13.250
So, knowing whether or not
one is a sage eater or

00:08:13.251 --> 00:08:15.070
a thyme eater permits

00:08:15.071 --> 00:08:18.420
better classification, more
accurate classification.

00:08:18.421 --> 00:08:24.090
&gt;&gt; Now, as you all know,

00:08:24.091 --> 00:08:27.990
in machine learning we
train on historical data.

00:08:28.920 --> 00:08:32.500
Historical data is often biased,

00:08:32.501 --> 00:08:36.260
and so we can't just say train
on the data because we'll

00:08:36.261 --> 00:08:40.470
imbibe the biases in the
mother's milk of training data.

00:08:40.471 --> 00:08:43.040
This brings up
a really important problem,

00:08:43.041 --> 00:08:45.720
which is that in general,

00:08:45.721 --> 00:08:48.250
there is no general source

00:08:48.251 --> 00:08:51.365
of ground truth for the things
that we're trying to do.

00:08:51.366 --> 00:08:54.845
So, we have to figure out

00:08:54.846 --> 00:08:58.355
how to extract fairness
when we possibly can,

00:08:58.356 --> 00:08:59.855
keeping this in mind.

00:08:59.856 --> 00:09:01.930
We can't just go to
the training data.

00:09:01.931 --> 00:09:08.445
Now, who knows what
this is a picture of?

00:09:08.446 --> 00:09:10.860
Okay. All right.

00:09:10.861 --> 00:09:13.450
This is a picture from
the Sistine Chapel,

00:09:13.451 --> 00:09:18.345
it's Michelangelo's depiction
of the last judgment.

00:09:18.346 --> 00:09:21.235
So, we have Jesus in the middle,

00:09:21.236 --> 00:09:22.730
and on his left are the people

00:09:22.731 --> 00:09:24.200
who are being condemned to hell,

00:09:24.201 --> 00:09:25.490
and on the right are

00:09:25.491 --> 00:09:26.935
people who are going
to go to heaven.

00:09:26.936 --> 00:09:32.880
So, this is a binary
classification situation.

00:09:32.881 --> 00:09:36.600
You can have a ternary
classification situation.

00:09:36.601 --> 00:09:41.230
So, here, we have some slides
from brain tissue.

00:09:41.231 --> 00:09:42.900
Over on the left,

00:09:42.901 --> 00:09:45.345
this is cancer, on the right,

00:09:45.346 --> 00:09:46.975
is healthy brain tissue,

00:09:46.976 --> 00:09:51.515
and in the middle we have
cancerous cells on the left.

00:09:51.516 --> 00:09:53.340
These cells, they're not

00:09:53.341 --> 00:09:56.380
cancerous but they're
not normal either,

00:09:56.381 --> 00:09:58.800
they're affected by the tumor.

00:09:58.801 --> 00:10:00.920
So, we might want a ternary,

00:10:00.921 --> 00:10:02.500
a more complicated classification

00:10:02.501 --> 00:10:05.020
system for something like this.

00:10:05.021 --> 00:10:09.220
Here's another
classification problem.

00:10:09.221 --> 00:10:12.950
I'm calling this a dependent
classification problem.

00:10:12.951 --> 00:10:17.115
So, let's say that you're
trying to hire people.

00:10:17.116 --> 00:10:19.040
You're job is not just to

00:10:19.041 --> 00:10:22.100
distinguish the qualified
from the unqualified,

00:10:22.101 --> 00:10:24.125
or even gradations among that,

00:10:24.126 --> 00:10:26.390
you have a more
complicated problem.

00:10:26.391 --> 00:10:32.290
Maybe there are hundreds

00:10:32.291 --> 00:10:34.920
of qualified people and
well-qualified people,

00:10:34.921 --> 00:10:38.135
but you only want to
interview 10 of them because,

00:10:38.136 --> 00:10:40.150
after all, you have
finite resources.

00:10:40.151 --> 00:10:42.900
So, you want to fairly

00:10:42.901 --> 00:10:45.975
select a cohort of
people to interview.

00:10:45.976 --> 00:10:49.390
So, that's a classification
problem where you can't just

00:10:49.391 --> 00:10:51.949
classify each person individually

00:10:51.950 --> 00:10:53.760
as to whether they're
good enough to interview,

00:10:53.761 --> 00:10:56.580
you have to somehow constrain it.

00:10:57.020 --> 00:11:06.330
Another kind of classification
problem is scoring.

00:11:15.530 --> 00:11:20.940
This is something for
evaluating arrested children to

00:11:20.941 --> 00:11:23.790
determine whether or
not they should be

00:11:23.791 --> 00:11:26.890
eligible for
detention or release,

00:11:26.891 --> 00:11:29.070
whether they should be
detained or released.

00:11:29.071 --> 00:11:31.130
So, it's a scoring system.

00:11:31.131 --> 00:11:33.060
You can see sorts of things like,

00:11:33.061 --> 00:11:35.720
there are points for
the various kinds of offenses

00:11:35.721 --> 00:11:38.625
that they committed
this particular time.

00:11:38.626 --> 00:11:40.690
Then, there are
other sorts of points

00:11:40.691 --> 00:11:42.830
for their prior offense history.

00:11:42.831 --> 00:11:45.890
There are negative points

00:11:45.891 --> 00:11:49.550
that come from
mitigating factors.

00:11:49.850 --> 00:11:58.220
These scores are then converted
into a binary decision,

00:11:58.221 --> 00:12:03.010
whether to release or to detain.

00:12:03.011 --> 00:12:05.680
Depending on the definition
that you're using,

00:12:05.681 --> 00:12:09.210
fairness in scoring
will or will not result

00:12:09.211 --> 00:12:13.860
in fairness in the decision.

00:12:15.420 --> 00:12:18.135
I still haven't defined anything.

00:12:18.136 --> 00:12:21.560
Now, we want to

00:12:21.561 --> 00:12:24.250
have a mathematically
rigorous approach

00:12:24.251 --> 00:12:26.280
to algorithmic fairness.

00:12:26.281 --> 00:12:30.170
Historically, there's
a way of trying to

00:12:30.171 --> 00:12:33.550
take these big goals and

00:12:33.551 --> 00:12:36.510
translate them into
a research program

00:12:36.511 --> 00:12:40.915
that should lead to
some practicable outcome.

00:12:40.916 --> 00:12:46.800
So, the first item in the
basic paradigm is definitions.

00:12:46.801 --> 00:12:48.690
What are you trying to do?

00:12:48.691 --> 00:12:50.280
In our case, this is going to be,

00:12:50.281 --> 00:12:51.795
what do we mean by fair.

00:12:51.796 --> 00:12:57.485
So, one way of doing this is that

00:12:57.486 --> 00:13:00.110
you can define
fairness by thinking

00:13:00.111 --> 00:13:03.410
about what it is you're
trying to prevent.

00:13:03.411 --> 00:13:06.160
So, in a good
cryptographic tradition,

00:13:06.161 --> 00:13:08.880
we imagine an adversary,

00:13:08.881 --> 00:13:10.995
and the adversary
is trying to force

00:13:10.996 --> 00:13:13.950
unfairness and you want
to defend against this.

00:13:13.951 --> 00:13:17.740
So, what are the various
behaviors for goals that

00:13:17.741 --> 00:13:23.170
the adversary may have that
you want to defend against?

00:13:23.790 --> 00:13:26.389
So, you define fairness.

00:13:26.390 --> 00:13:28.270
Then, what you want to do,

00:13:28.271 --> 00:13:29.770
start with a definition, then you

00:13:29.771 --> 00:13:32.210
want to create algorithms,

00:13:32.211 --> 00:13:35.010
and you want that
your algorithms will

00:13:35.011 --> 00:13:38.265
enforce whatever this thing
is that you've defined.

00:13:38.266 --> 00:13:40.400
So, once we have
the definition of fairness,

00:13:40.401 --> 00:13:42.970
then we want to build a
classification algorithm

00:13:42.971 --> 00:13:46.900
that we'll be fair according
to that definition.

00:13:46.980 --> 00:13:51.945
Finally, we need a
composition theorem.

00:13:51.946 --> 00:13:57.100
So, think about the systems

00:13:57.101 --> 00:14:00.525
that you might use
that classify people.

00:14:00.526 --> 00:14:02.360
Let's say, you're
determining whether they're

00:14:02.361 --> 00:14:05.205
appropriate for
this ad or that ad.

00:14:05.206 --> 00:14:07.140
You don't just
classify them once,

00:14:07.141 --> 00:14:09.000
you classify them over, and over,

00:14:09.001 --> 00:14:11.885
and over again for all sorts
of different kinds of ads,

00:14:11.886 --> 00:14:13.840
and many different times of day,

00:14:13.841 --> 00:14:15.735
and all sorts of
things like that.

00:14:15.736 --> 00:14:17.620
Similarly, if you're doing

00:14:17.621 --> 00:14:19.765
a privacy preserving
data analysis,

00:14:19.766 --> 00:14:22.780
nobody just wants one privacy
preserving statistic,

00:14:22.781 --> 00:14:25.125
they want a whole
ton of statistics.

00:14:25.126 --> 00:14:27.070
Cryptography, you don't just see

00:14:27.071 --> 00:14:30.190
one message or one does
digital signature,

00:14:30.191 --> 00:14:31.600
you've got digital signatures on

00:14:31.601 --> 00:14:33.110
tons of different messages,

00:14:33.111 --> 00:14:35.400
and encryptions of tons
of different messages.

00:14:35.401 --> 00:14:38.760
So, you need to prove some sort
of theorem that talks

00:14:38.761 --> 00:14:42.369
about how these things
combine in practice,

00:14:42.370 --> 00:14:44.595
and those are called
composition theorems.

00:14:44.596 --> 00:14:46.050
So, here, what we would

00:14:46.051 --> 00:14:47.570
want is we would want
to say that if you

00:14:47.571 --> 00:14:50.400
have a system based on you
know made a fair pieces,

00:14:50.401 --> 00:14:53.690
then the system somehow
is fair in total.

00:14:53.691 --> 00:14:58.685
So, this is a general paradigm
that has been successful,

00:14:58.686 --> 00:15:02.330
particularly in cryptography
and in privacy.

00:15:02.331 --> 00:15:06.625
So, can we apply
this to fairness?

00:15:06.626 --> 00:15:08.735
The short answer is,

00:15:08.736 --> 00:15:12.420
"I think we probably can
but it's way harder in

00:15:12.421 --> 00:15:16.830
my experience than in
the other scenarios."

00:15:16.831 --> 00:15:19.840
Defining fairness is very,

00:15:19.841 --> 00:15:23.660
very difficult and very,
very controversial.

00:15:24.800 --> 00:15:27.810
I don't believe it's
going to be possible to

00:15:27.811 --> 00:15:30.325
come up with a single definition

00:15:30.326 --> 00:15:36.900
that the technical community

00:15:36.901 --> 00:15:39.280
can come up with
a single definition that

00:15:39.281 --> 00:15:42.060
the population as a whole
would say, "Yes that's it."

00:15:42.061 --> 00:15:44.900
I don't think that's
going to happen.

00:15:46.340 --> 00:15:49.040
&gt;&gt; Building algorithms
that's our bread and

00:15:49.041 --> 00:15:50.760
butter once we have definitions.

00:15:50.761 --> 00:15:52.200
There's been a lot
of work already

00:15:52.201 --> 00:15:53.320
on building algorithms,

00:15:53.321 --> 00:15:56.155
some of it very recent
and very exciting.

00:15:56.156 --> 00:15:58.205
So I'll talk
a little bit about that,

00:15:58.206 --> 00:16:01.730
and composition turns
out to be very tricky

00:16:01.731 --> 00:16:06.250
and so we'll see a little bit
about that as well.

00:16:06.251 --> 00:16:09.660
So let's talk about
defining fairness.

00:16:09.661 --> 00:16:12.760
I said that we

00:16:12.761 --> 00:16:16.485
think about what it is
an adversary is trying to do.

00:16:16.486 --> 00:16:19.980
So think about for example,
digital signature schemes.

00:16:19.981 --> 00:16:23.000
What do you want from
a digital signature scheme?

00:16:23.001 --> 00:16:25.760
You're all going to say
the same thing right away.

00:16:25.761 --> 00:16:27.975
Nobody should be able to forge,

00:16:27.976 --> 00:16:30.610
then you have to get into
some details about all of

00:16:30.611 --> 00:16:32.420
the right quantifiers
about what does it

00:16:32.421 --> 00:16:34.385
mean to say nobody
should be able to forge,

00:16:34.386 --> 00:16:37.275
but forgeries are what
you're trying to prevent.

00:16:37.276 --> 00:16:41.105
So, what are we trying
to prevent here?

00:16:41.106 --> 00:16:44.540
So one of our starting scenarios

00:16:44.541 --> 00:16:47.305
was actually in the context
of advertising.

00:16:47.306 --> 00:16:49.420
So, advertisers might be

00:16:49.421 --> 00:16:52.940
biased and we want to
build a platform that will

00:16:52.941 --> 00:16:58.750
mitigate the biases of
the advertisers and be fair.

00:16:58.751 --> 00:17:02.650
So, we literally sat
around and said, "Okay,

00:17:02.651 --> 00:17:04.500
what are some of
the kinds of things,

00:17:04.501 --> 00:17:09.055
a really nasty ill-intentioned
advertiser might do?"

00:17:09.056 --> 00:17:14.290
So red lining we talked
about and that's real.

00:17:14.291 --> 00:17:18.750
Red lining in loans
was absolutely real.

00:17:18.751 --> 00:17:21.340
Housing loans for example
in the United States.

00:17:21.341 --> 00:17:23.360
There's a fabulous book
you should read

00:17:23.361 --> 00:17:25.945
by Richard Rothstein
called, The Color of Law,

00:17:25.946 --> 00:17:28.710
which talks about the effect of

00:17:28.711 --> 00:17:32.965
the US government
in these biases.

00:17:32.966 --> 00:17:35.540
There's reverse tokenism.

00:17:35.541 --> 00:17:38.380
So let's say for example,

00:17:39.500 --> 00:17:42.120
you turn me down
for a loan I say,

00:17:42.121 --> 00:17:44.280
"Oh you turned me down
because I have curly

00:17:44.281 --> 00:17:48.010
hair," and they point
to Sergei and they say,

00:17:48.011 --> 00:17:50.710
"Obviously, he's way more
qualified than you are.

00:17:50.711 --> 00:17:53.630
He has straight hair and
we turned him down too."

00:17:53.631 --> 00:17:55.730
The thing is they're using him as

00:17:55.731 --> 00:17:58.080
the example for all of
the curly haired people.

00:17:58.081 --> 00:18:01.065
So that's a reverse tokenism.

00:18:01.066 --> 00:18:03.640
Then there's what
I'm going to call

00:18:03.641 --> 00:18:06.540
deliberately targeting
the wrong subset of

00:18:06.541 --> 00:18:08.435
the sage eaters and
I'm going to actually

00:18:08.436 --> 00:18:12.815
postpone that example
for a slid or two.

00:18:12.816 --> 00:18:16.045
So we literally just
sat down and wrote

00:18:16.046 --> 00:18:18.270
a whole bunch of negative things

00:18:18.271 --> 00:18:20.635
that we wanted to try to prevent.

00:18:20.636 --> 00:18:23.120
Is this a reasonable approach?

00:18:23.121 --> 00:18:24.380
Well, I don't know

00:18:24.381 --> 00:18:28.800
another one and one of
the advantages with

00:18:28.801 --> 00:18:31.640
this sort of approach is
let's say that you build

00:18:31.641 --> 00:18:33.060
a system that protects

00:18:33.061 --> 00:18:35.045
against all these things
that we've listed.

00:18:35.046 --> 00:18:38.200
That's nice. Then somebody
comes along and says,

00:18:38.201 --> 00:18:40.340
"You missed one,"
you say, "Great."

00:18:40.341 --> 00:18:42.140
Now you redesign your system to

00:18:42.141 --> 00:18:44.010
protect against
the new one as well.

00:18:44.011 --> 00:18:46.020
In the meantime,
you've been protecting

00:18:46.021 --> 00:18:48.215
against these first
behaviors anyway,

00:18:48.216 --> 00:18:52.110
so something happened that
was better than nothing.

00:18:56.560 --> 00:18:59.500
When most people think

00:18:59.501 --> 00:19:02.070
about fairness the way
I've introduced it,

00:19:02.071 --> 00:19:04.530
they'll immediately think
in terms of groups and

00:19:04.531 --> 00:19:07.285
in fact the way I introduced
it with suggestive of that.

00:19:07.286 --> 00:19:10.250
So what are group fair?

00:19:10.251 --> 00:19:12.600
Group fairness properties
are basically

00:19:12.601 --> 00:19:14.430
statistical requirements

00:19:14.431 --> 00:19:17.010
about the treatment
of different groups.

00:19:17.011 --> 00:19:20.460
So statistical parity,
which is also

00:19:20.461 --> 00:19:24.255
known as demographic
parenting, says that,

00:19:24.256 --> 00:19:26.880
let's say if we're just
doing binary classification

00:19:26.881 --> 00:19:28.095
positive and negative,

00:19:28.096 --> 00:19:31.355
it says that the demographics
of the people with

00:19:31.356 --> 00:19:34.690
positive classification
should be the same as

00:19:34.691 --> 00:19:38.250
the demographics of
the general population.

00:19:38.251 --> 00:19:40.700
So the same proportions are

00:19:40.701 --> 00:19:43.000
being positively
classified for sage eaters

00:19:43.001 --> 00:19:45.130
and time eaters as
in the population as

00:19:45.131 --> 00:19:46.710
a whole and also the same

00:19:46.711 --> 00:19:48.850
for the negative classifications.

00:19:48.851 --> 00:19:52.610
Now, this is nice.

00:19:53.810 --> 00:19:57.740
When is it really meaningful?

00:19:57.741 --> 00:20:01.210
As we'll see, it's mostly
meaningful in the breach.

00:20:01.211 --> 00:20:04.555
If you see some
incredible departure

00:20:04.556 --> 00:20:07.040
from statistical parity,

00:20:07.041 --> 00:20:08.980
that's a red flag.

00:20:08.981 --> 00:20:12.055
Doesn't mean that things have
been unfair necessarily,

00:20:12.056 --> 00:20:15.460
but it certainly is a nice place
to go looking to see,

00:20:15.461 --> 00:20:17.535
why did this happen?

00:20:17.536 --> 00:20:20.890
So, it can be meaningful in

00:20:20.891 --> 00:20:24.205
the breach but it
permits what we call,

00:20:24.206 --> 00:20:26.250
as I said targeting
the wrong subset

00:20:26.251 --> 00:20:28.735
of S. So I have a spa,

00:20:28.736 --> 00:20:30.925
it's a luxurious spa and

00:20:30.926 --> 00:20:34.610
I really don't want
those sage eaters at my spa,

00:20:34.611 --> 00:20:37.140
but I have to
advertise to them in

00:20:37.141 --> 00:20:38.330
the same proportion as

00:20:38.331 --> 00:20:40.685
their proportion in
the population as a whole.

00:20:40.686 --> 00:20:44.335
So what do I do?
How do I be biased?

00:20:44.336 --> 00:20:48.470
I advertise to the members

00:20:48.471 --> 00:20:52.385
of S who can't afford
to come to my spa.

00:20:52.386 --> 00:20:54.840
So I've deliberately targeting

00:20:54.841 --> 00:20:57.080
the ones who can't come and

00:20:57.081 --> 00:20:59.610
therefore I'm ensuring that

00:20:59.611 --> 00:21:03.235
my spa isn't gaining
new sage eaters.

00:21:03.236 --> 00:21:05.150
But among the time eaters,

00:21:05.151 --> 00:21:08.630
I advertise to the wealthy
and they can come.

00:21:08.631 --> 00:21:11.550
So demographic
parody, oops it's not

00:21:11.551 --> 00:21:15.120
enough and you have to look
at it much more carefully.

00:21:19.770 --> 00:21:26.270
There's also this question
of the intersectional cases,

00:21:26.271 --> 00:21:28.180
which leads to what's

00:21:28.181 --> 00:21:30.970
colorfully called
fairness gerrymandering.

00:21:30.971 --> 00:21:33.700
So, we have our population.

00:21:33.701 --> 00:21:37.105
I've divide it into the
sage eaters and the time eaters,

00:21:37.106 --> 00:21:39.050
but they're also the tea drinkers

00:21:39.051 --> 00:21:40.910
and the coffee drinkers.

00:21:40.911 --> 00:21:44.110
What we maybe really want is

00:21:44.111 --> 00:21:45.220
that if we look at

00:21:45.221 --> 00:21:47.785
the four different
intersection categories,

00:21:47.786 --> 00:21:49.980
we should have
proportional representation

00:21:49.981 --> 00:21:51.960
for each of these categories.

00:21:51.961 --> 00:21:55.020
It would be very unfair
if we only showed

00:21:55.021 --> 00:21:58.295
the ad to sage eating
coffee drinkers.

00:21:58.296 --> 00:22:01.210
So you can fill in
demographic groups for where you

00:22:01.211 --> 00:22:05.310
really clearly want
this intersectional parody.

00:22:05.880 --> 00:22:09.090
Statistical parody by itself does

00:22:09.091 --> 00:22:15.440
not protect against this kind
of fairness gerrymandering.

00:22:32.270 --> 00:22:34.750
So statistical parody was

00:22:34.751 --> 00:22:37.465
one definition of
fairness for groups,

00:22:37.466 --> 00:22:39.130
there are other
definitions of fairness

00:22:39.131 --> 00:22:40.720
for groups and then we have to

00:22:40.721 --> 00:22:42.140
face the fact that

00:22:42.141 --> 00:22:46.925
some group fairness properties
are mutually incompatible.

00:22:46.926 --> 00:22:50.110
So how many of you
know this story?

00:22:50.970 --> 00:22:54.350
So many of you do but
most of you don't.

00:22:54.351 --> 00:22:58.920
So, I have a classifier.

00:23:00.110 --> 00:23:02.370
So let's say that we

00:23:02.371 --> 00:23:07.360
are classifying people
and we are predicting.

00:23:07.361 --> 00:23:08.970
So we're classifying as to

00:23:08.971 --> 00:23:11.340
whether we think you're
going to do something,

00:23:11.341 --> 00:23:13.590
we think you're
going to recidivate

00:23:13.591 --> 00:23:16.415
versus we don't think
you're going to recidivate.

00:23:16.416 --> 00:23:18.325
So importantly here,

00:23:18.326 --> 00:23:20.605
there's information that's
going to be missing,

00:23:20.606 --> 00:23:22.520
like the future hasn't happened

00:23:22.521 --> 00:23:25.360
yet and whether
someone released from

00:23:25.361 --> 00:23:27.580
jail recidivates may
depend on many things that

00:23:27.581 --> 00:23:30.940
happened to them during
their experiences.

00:23:30.941 --> 00:23:32.300
So one thing you might

00:23:32.301 --> 00:23:35.270
want reasonably is
that you would have

00:23:35.271 --> 00:23:38.675
an equal false positive rates

00:23:38.676 --> 00:23:41.375
across your different
demographic groups.

00:23:41.376 --> 00:23:43.230
Another might be
that you would want

00:23:43.231 --> 00:23:44.805
an equal false negative rates

00:23:44.806 --> 00:23:47.330
across the different
demographic groups.

00:23:47.331 --> 00:23:48.920
You might also want

00:23:48.921 --> 00:23:51.340
equal positive predictive
value which is

00:23:51.341 --> 00:23:53.910
among those who are
predicted to recidivate,

00:23:53.911 --> 00:23:55.300
how many of them actually do?

00:23:55.301 --> 00:23:57.850
That's the positive
predictive value.

00:23:58.080 --> 00:24:00.580
I chose these three,
these aren't my

00:24:00.581 --> 00:24:02.620
only three but these
are very famous,

00:24:02.621 --> 00:24:04.600
because it's known that

00:24:04.601 --> 00:24:09.020
no imperfect classifier
can simultaneously

00:24:09.021 --> 00:24:12.505
ensure equal false positive
rates across groups

00:24:12.506 --> 00:24:14.350
and equal false negative rates

00:24:14.351 --> 00:24:16.895
and equal positive
predictive value,

00:24:16.896 --> 00:24:22.230
unless the base rate is
the same across groups.

00:24:22.231 --> 00:24:23.450
So unless the base rate of

00:24:23.451 --> 00:24:27.000
recidivating is the
same across groups.

00:24:28.930 --> 00:24:31.110
The reason for this,

00:24:31.111 --> 00:24:33.485
as Chouldechova points
out in her paper,

00:24:33.486 --> 00:24:37.130
is that these quantities are
related by this equation.

00:24:37.131 --> 00:24:40.270
So we could write down for

00:24:40.271 --> 00:24:43.170
the sage eaters
the false positive rate,

00:24:43.171 --> 00:24:44.360
the false negative rate and

00:24:44.361 --> 00:24:47.530
the positive predictive value
and they would satisfy

00:24:47.531 --> 00:24:49.090
this equation where P would be

00:24:49.091 --> 00:24:52.570
the base rate among
the sage eaters.

00:24:52.571 --> 00:24:54.030
We would then write down

00:24:54.031 --> 00:24:56.715
the same equation
for the time eaters.

00:24:56.716 --> 00:25:00.755
Now, requiring
equal false positive rates

00:25:00.756 --> 00:25:02.910
across the two groups means that

00:25:02.911 --> 00:25:09.650
this term would have to be the
same in the two equations.

00:25:09.651 --> 00:25:12.110
So then these blue guys

00:25:12.111 --> 00:25:13.170
have to be the same and that

00:25:13.171 --> 00:25:14.250
blue term would have to be the

00:25:14.251 --> 00:25:16.430
same and the only way
this could happen is

00:25:16.431 --> 00:25:19.980
if the base rate P is the
same in the two groups.

00:25:28.320 --> 00:25:31.445
When the future is unknown,

00:25:31.446 --> 00:25:34.660
so therefore classification
would be imperfect,

00:25:34.661 --> 00:25:38.220
no algorithm can
simultaneously ensure

00:25:38.221 --> 00:25:39.620
these three very natural to

00:25:39.621 --> 00:25:43.120
desiderata unless
the base rate is the same.

00:25:43.121 --> 00:25:48.985
Nobody cares how
that algorithm is implemented.

00:25:48.986 --> 00:25:53.000
So if you believe in
free choice or randomness,

00:25:53.001 --> 00:25:56.865
you get the same thing
whether the algorithm is

00:25:56.866 --> 00:26:01.940
a machine or whether
it's some other form.

00:26:02.850 --> 00:26:06.940
&gt;&gt; So, it does not make sense

00:26:06.941 --> 00:26:08.480
to say let's solve this problem

00:26:08.481 --> 00:26:10.815
by bringing a human
into the loop.

00:26:10.816 --> 00:26:13.000
Does nobody could bring into

00:26:13.001 --> 00:26:15.825
the loop that would
address this problem.

00:26:15.826 --> 00:26:18.820
It's just the math.

00:26:20.820 --> 00:26:24.345
So as you can probably tell,

00:26:24.346 --> 00:26:28.540
I find a group fairness notions

00:26:29.900 --> 00:26:32.950
dissatisfying for
a number of reasons.

00:26:32.951 --> 00:26:35.085
I'm sharing some of
these reasons with you.

00:26:35.086 --> 00:26:41.405
Now this brings us to
a very famous video.

00:26:41.406 --> 00:26:43.605
How many people have seen this?

00:26:43.606 --> 00:26:47.090
Not all, great.
I mean not even half.

00:26:47.091 --> 00:26:50.160
So, this is an experiment

00:26:50.161 --> 00:26:53.110
with Capuchin Monkeys,
they're paired up.

00:26:53.111 --> 00:26:56.160
The monkey has to
do a task which is

00:26:56.161 --> 00:26:59.925
to hand you a rock and then
the monkey gets a reward.

00:26:59.926 --> 00:27:06.470
So, let's see, sorry.

00:27:07.080 --> 00:27:10.870
&gt;&gt; So, she gives a rock
to us that's the task.

00:27:10.871 --> 00:27:13.660
We give our piece of
Cucumber and she eats it.

00:27:13.661 --> 00:27:16.225
The other one needs
to give a rock to us,

00:27:16.226 --> 00:27:18.810
and that's what she does.

00:27:18.811 --> 00:27:20.670
She gets a Grape.

00:27:20.671 --> 00:27:24.305
If the other one sees that,

00:27:24.306 --> 00:27:28.260
she gives a rock to us
now gets again Cucumber.

00:27:41.960 --> 00:27:46.510
&gt;&gt; Okay. So that brings us to

00:27:46.511 --> 00:27:50.835
the notion that I prefer
which is Individual Fairness.

00:27:50.836 --> 00:27:55.955
Individual Fairness
says intuitively that,

00:27:55.956 --> 00:27:58.230
"For a given classification task,

00:27:58.231 --> 00:28:01.700
if two people are
similar with respect to

00:28:01.701 --> 00:28:04.080
that classification task then

00:28:04.081 --> 00:28:06.270
they should be
treated similarly."

00:28:06.271 --> 00:28:09.420
Now to make any sense
out of this,

00:28:09.421 --> 00:28:11.500
we need the right
notion of what it

00:28:11.501 --> 00:28:13.635
means to be similar
or dissimilar.

00:28:13.636 --> 00:28:15.760
We need some kind of
a distance notion.

00:28:15.761 --> 00:28:17.880
So, a classic example is in

00:28:17.881 --> 00:28:21.095
credit scoring where
the difference in credit scoring

00:28:21.096 --> 00:28:25.225
literally gives you
a metric for how

00:28:25.226 --> 00:28:27.870
dissimilar a pair of
people are considered to

00:28:27.871 --> 00:28:30.945
be for credit worthiness.

00:28:30.946 --> 00:28:39.485
Okay. Of course, again,

00:28:39.486 --> 00:28:43.115
I have to emphasize this
is a Task-Specific Notion.

00:28:43.116 --> 00:28:44.810
So, Sergei and I might be in

00:28:44.811 --> 00:28:46.670
fact comparably qualified for

00:28:46.671 --> 00:28:48.660
a loan but for recommending

00:28:48.661 --> 00:28:50.930
hair products who would
be completely different.

00:28:50.931 --> 00:28:52.900
So we're similar
for one purpose but

00:28:52.901 --> 00:28:55.965
totally different
for another purpose.

00:28:55.966 --> 00:28:58.880
So, it's very important
that when we talk about

00:28:58.881 --> 00:29:01.430
these metrics they're
Task-Specific.

00:29:01.431 --> 00:29:05.035
Now, thinking about
credit scores,

00:29:05.036 --> 00:29:08.510
maybe people in
general are sort of

00:29:08.511 --> 00:29:10.710
smushed uniformly from highly

00:29:10.711 --> 00:29:12.870
qualified to very unqualified.

00:29:12.871 --> 00:29:23.160
So, when you're trying

00:29:23.161 --> 00:29:25.010
to decide whether to
give a loan based

00:29:25.011 --> 00:29:27.045
on whether somebody
crosses a threshold,

00:29:27.046 --> 00:29:30.600
you may have two people
who are very close and you

00:29:30.601 --> 00:29:32.060
give one of them the loan and you

00:29:32.061 --> 00:29:34.730
deny it to the other one, right?

00:29:34.731 --> 00:29:36.560
If the scores are in fact sort

00:29:36.561 --> 00:29:40.750
of densely packed
along this line,

00:29:40.751 --> 00:29:42.475
then indeed that has to happen.

00:29:42.476 --> 00:29:45.015
So, you sort of have to
draw the line somewhere,

00:29:45.016 --> 00:29:47.170
that's the general worry.

00:29:47.171 --> 00:29:49.150
Then you would have
very similar people

00:29:49.151 --> 00:29:51.255
who are being treated
very dissimilarly.

00:29:51.256 --> 00:29:54.800
So, what do the theoreticians
say that you should do in

00:29:54.801 --> 00:29:58.750
this case? You randomize.

00:29:58.751 --> 00:30:02.060
So, instead of deciding yes

00:30:02.061 --> 00:30:05.745
definitely for you and
definitely not for you,

00:30:05.746 --> 00:30:08.180
you assign to people
a probability of

00:30:08.181 --> 00:30:10.160
getting the loan or
a probability of

00:30:10.161 --> 00:30:18.410
getting an A depending on
their creditworthiness.

00:30:18.670 --> 00:30:23.065
You arrange that if
two people are very similar,

00:30:23.066 --> 00:30:25.720
then the probability
distribution on outcomes

00:30:25.721 --> 00:30:29.455
that they see is
very similar. Yes?

00:30:29.456 --> 00:30:34.040
&gt;&gt; Two policemen were just
fired for tossing a coin to

00:30:34.041 --> 00:30:37.940
decide whether to arrest
someone for speeding ticket.

00:30:37.941 --> 00:30:42.250
So, they may have
heard your lecture.

00:30:43.130 --> 00:30:46.205
&gt;&gt; No. I say is

00:30:46.206 --> 00:30:48.340
there are circumstances under

00:30:48.341 --> 00:30:49.890
which it might be
the right thing to do.

00:30:49.891 --> 00:30:53.190
Police have huge amounts
of discretion,

00:30:53.191 --> 00:30:56.110
and if they treat

00:30:56.111 --> 00:30:59.400
similar people similarly
maybe that's fine.

00:30:59.401 --> 00:31:01.780
&gt;&gt; But maybe instead
of randomizing,

00:31:01.781 --> 00:31:04.050
one could just scale the loans.

00:31:04.051 --> 00:31:06.810
So instead of having
a fixed amount that you then,

00:31:06.811 --> 00:31:11.835
you could change the amount
of loan until reaches zero.

00:31:11.836 --> 00:31:15.110
&gt;&gt; You could certainly
do something like that.

00:31:15.111 --> 00:31:18.820
I would have to think through
all of the ramifications.

00:31:18.821 --> 00:31:21.120
But, there are cases

00:31:21.121 --> 00:31:23.275
where you must make
a binary decision.

00:31:23.276 --> 00:31:27.890
So, we're going to

00:31:27.891 --> 00:31:30.730
take this as our
definition of Fairness.

00:31:30.731 --> 00:31:32.850
So, we have a set of outcomes.

00:31:32.851 --> 00:31:35.475
In this case, it's
yes or no that's O.

00:31:35.476 --> 00:31:38.860
This is the probability
distributions on outcomes.

00:31:38.861 --> 00:31:40.780
So, we want to find
a classifier that

00:31:40.781 --> 00:31:43.154
maps individuals in our universe

00:31:43.155 --> 00:31:44.800
to probability distributions on

00:31:44.801 --> 00:31:47.970
outcomes with
the following condition.

00:31:47.971 --> 00:31:51.970
For some appropriate measure

00:31:51.971 --> 00:31:55.190
of the difference of
two probability distributions,

00:31:55.191 --> 00:31:57.530
for example total
variation distance,

00:31:57.531 --> 00:31:59.835
we want that the difference

00:31:59.836 --> 00:32:01.140
in these distributions will be

00:32:01.141 --> 00:32:06.504
bounded by the distance
of these two individuals.

00:32:06.505 --> 00:32:08.400
So, it's a Lipschitz constraint,

00:32:08.401 --> 00:32:13.155
the distance under the appropriate
tasks specific metric.

00:32:13.156 --> 00:32:15.870
Now, of course this immediately

00:32:15.871 --> 00:32:18.940
says where does
the metric come from?

00:32:18.941 --> 00:32:22.680
How are you going to get
your hands on the metric.

00:32:23.340 --> 00:32:26.420
I don't have
a great answer to that,

00:32:26.421 --> 00:32:30.425
but I also believe

00:32:30.426 --> 00:32:33.220
that if we actually had

00:32:33.221 --> 00:32:36.835
a classification system that
people thought was fair,

00:32:36.836 --> 00:32:41.210
then we could extract
from it a metric.

00:32:41.650 --> 00:32:45.640
&gt;&gt; So, how do we ensure
that the metric is fair?

00:32:45.641 --> 00:32:48.720
&gt;&gt; That's exactly what
I just said,

00:32:48.810 --> 00:32:51.990
we don't know where to get
our hands on the metrics.

00:32:51.991 --> 00:32:54.000
So, when we did
this work initially,

00:32:54.001 --> 00:32:58.480
we said that at the very
best it was going to be

00:32:58.481 --> 00:33:00.170
societies best guess at

00:33:00.171 --> 00:33:03.645
the moment in clearly
what we would need.

00:33:03.646 --> 00:33:05.980
I'm now understanding
more and more that

00:33:05.981 --> 00:33:07.370
these things are
going to be handled

00:33:07.371 --> 00:33:08.775
by political processes,

00:33:08.776 --> 00:33:14.760
that there's no other way
of getting these things

00:33:14.761 --> 00:33:18.130
other than combining stakeholders

00:33:18.131 --> 00:33:21.590
and measurements
to get somewhere.

00:33:21.591 --> 00:33:24.400
But we don't have
a straight mathematical answer

00:33:24.401 --> 00:33:27.695
for where we're going
to get the metric. Yes?

00:33:27.696 --> 00:33:29.965
&gt;&gt; Is the sort of tension between

00:33:29.966 --> 00:33:34.100
&gt;&gt; Who watches their
watchmen as an argument

00:33:34.101 --> 00:33:37.750
or the best person to decide
if I'm going to offend you,

00:33:37.751 --> 00:33:39.280
is you not me.

00:33:39.281 --> 00:33:41.950
It doesn't mean the rule
of fairness is it to

00:33:41.951 --> 00:33:45.750
be a case that in society
will dictate that,

00:33:45.751 --> 00:33:47.555
we'll go back into
the group fairness.

00:33:47.556 --> 00:33:50.315
So, for individual fairness,

00:33:50.316 --> 00:33:53.395
the toughest nut to crack
is that the source of

00:33:53.396 --> 00:33:56.760
the metric is the subject

00:33:56.761 --> 00:33:59.380
to which the decision
will be applied.

00:33:59.381 --> 00:34:00.905
As in the case of the monkey,

00:34:00.906 --> 00:34:03.130
the monkey was the one
decided, "This is not fair."

00:34:03.131 --> 00:34:07.060
So, any hands as to

00:34:07.061 --> 00:34:10.080
how to integral and

00:34:10.081 --> 00:34:12.680
other societal and
individual fairness

00:34:12.681 --> 00:34:15.290
or separate them properly?

00:34:15.291 --> 00:34:18.560
&gt;&gt; So, I don't have a
mathematical answer to that yet.

00:34:18.561 --> 00:34:22.690
What I'm starting to engage
in very heavily now is,

00:34:22.691 --> 00:34:26.180
what is the right way of
thinking about it politically.

00:34:26.181 --> 00:34:27.670
I don't mean
political correctness,

00:34:27.671 --> 00:34:30.820
I mean political
theory and ethics.

00:34:30.821 --> 00:34:34.480
But, it's an unknown,

00:34:34.481 --> 00:34:36.980
and work on it guys.

00:34:36.981 --> 00:34:38.610
It really makes sense,

00:34:38.611 --> 00:34:40.430
it's an important problem.

00:34:40.431 --> 00:34:42.370
Maybe ask me more
about it at the end.

00:34:42.371 --> 00:34:44.450
&gt;&gt; Are we right people
to work on it?

00:34:44.610 --> 00:34:47.495
&gt;&gt; So, not by yourselves,

00:34:47.496 --> 00:34:51.530
unless you have
a whole very broad education.

00:34:52.830 --> 00:34:56.270
Inevitably, there's going to be

00:34:56.271 --> 00:34:58.230
