trying to work in here

00:36:40.876 --> 00:36:42.150
like whether the brain

00:36:42.151 --> 00:36:44.320
stops on itself when
it gets itself,

00:36:44.321 --> 00:36:46.455
I don't know exactly.

00:36:46.456 --> 00:36:49.470
Maybe asked me after, I'm sorry.

00:36:50.020 --> 00:36:52.600
I'm going to go on. Let's talk

00:36:52.601 --> 00:36:55.055
a little bit about algorithms.

00:36:55.056 --> 00:36:58.970
So, first, as I said,

00:36:58.971 --> 00:37:01.440
these are challenges
that we are familiar.

00:37:01.441 --> 00:37:04.900
So, in the ad setting,

00:37:04.901 --> 00:37:07.600
we imagined the situation

00:37:07.601 --> 00:37:11.050
where we actually knew
our universe of individuals,

00:37:11.051 --> 00:37:12.635
and we even gave the

00:37:12.636 --> 00:37:16.420
advertiser the opportunity
to say for each

00:37:16.421 --> 00:37:18.990
individual and
each potential outcome

00:37:18.991 --> 00:37:20.300
how bad is it for

00:37:20.301 --> 00:37:22.610
this individual to be
mapped to that outcome.

00:37:22.611 --> 00:37:24.900
So, what's a loss
that's associated

00:37:24.901 --> 00:37:28.990
incurred by mapping
this individual to outcome, o.

00:37:29.160 --> 00:37:32.260
It's just something that we
are adding optionally to

00:37:32.261 --> 00:37:34.985
allow the vendors to
weigh in on things.

00:37:34.986 --> 00:37:38.400
Then, we assemble
the ingredients of

00:37:38.401 --> 00:37:41.210
the science fiction metric

00:37:41.211 --> 00:37:45.185
and the vendor's loss functions.

00:37:45.186 --> 00:37:47.290
We want to minimize

00:37:47.291 --> 00:37:50.555
the vendor's loss subject
to the fairness conditions.

00:37:50.556 --> 00:37:53.030
So, we're viewing the
loss functions as

00:37:53.031 --> 00:37:54.690
soft constraints and

00:37:54.691 --> 00:37:57.685
the theorists conditions
as hard constraints.

00:37:57.686 --> 00:37:59.665
This is just a linear program.

00:37:59.666 --> 00:38:02.205
So, in theory, this
can be solved,

00:38:02.206 --> 00:38:09.100
and in fact, there's
an interesting observation,

00:38:09.101 --> 00:38:11.760
which is sort of evocative
of those theorems

00:38:11.761 --> 00:38:15.755
about what you can't do when
the base rates are unequal.

00:38:15.756 --> 00:38:20.120
If you solve the fairness
linear program,

00:38:20.121 --> 00:38:23.755
you get a classifier that gives
you a statistical parity,

00:38:23.756 --> 00:38:25.685
if and only if,

00:38:25.686 --> 00:38:29.780
intuitively that
the distributions are the same.

00:38:29.781 --> 00:38:32.240
So, the Earth mover
distance between

00:38:32.241 --> 00:38:33.460
the uniform distribution on

00:38:33.461 --> 00:38:36.660
the sage eaters and
the time eaters is zero.

00:38:40.290 --> 00:38:42.550
So, that was something where we

00:38:42.551 --> 00:38:44.330
assumed that we knew
the whole universe,

00:38:44.331 --> 00:38:49.480
very recently Rothblum
and Yona have shown

00:38:49.481 --> 00:38:52.060
how to relax this a little bit
to what they're

00:38:52.061 --> 00:38:55.025
calling probably
approximate fairness,

00:38:55.026 --> 00:38:57.645
and then they get
generalizability.

00:38:57.646 --> 00:39:01.680
We didn't generalize
in our initial result.

00:39:01.681 --> 00:39:04.380
So, that's a 2018 result.

00:39:04.381 --> 00:39:06.200
Now, I mentioned
that there's been

00:39:06.201 --> 00:39:08.260
a lot of progress
in recent years.

00:39:08.261 --> 00:39:11.410
So, for example, Hardt, Price,

00:39:11.411 --> 00:39:14.680
and Srebro took
equal false-negative rate

00:39:14.681 --> 00:39:16.705
and equal false-positive rate,

00:39:16.706 --> 00:39:20.065
and optionally also equal
false-positive rate

00:39:20.066 --> 00:39:21.910
as a fairness requirement,

00:39:21.911 --> 00:39:23.400
and they showed how to

00:39:23.401 --> 00:39:29.765
convert unfair predictors
to fair predictors.

00:39:29.766 --> 00:39:32.075
Not for individual fairness,

00:39:32.076 --> 00:39:35.835
their notion of fairness,
these group fairness.

00:39:35.836 --> 00:39:40.525
Something that I think that
you guys might appreciate is,

00:39:40.526 --> 00:39:43.475
they did this all
in post-processing.

00:39:43.476 --> 00:39:45.280
So, as you know,

00:39:45.281 --> 00:39:47.430
if you're trying to
get a product group to

00:39:47.431 --> 00:39:49.905
use one of your tools,

00:39:49.906 --> 00:39:51.930
it's much better
if you don't have

00:39:51.931 --> 00:39:53.980
to interfere in their pipeline,

00:39:53.981 --> 00:39:56.930
and you can stick something
at the beginning or the end.

00:39:56.931 --> 00:40:00.140
So, that's something they did.

00:40:00.141 --> 00:40:03.000
Then, in other work,
Joseph Kearns,

00:40:03.001 --> 00:40:07.240
Morgan Stern, and Roth looked
at a different problem.

00:40:07.241 --> 00:40:09.060
They were looking at
a bandit setting.

00:40:09.061 --> 00:40:13.320
So, you have basically one arm

00:40:13.321 --> 00:40:14.670
for each demographic group.

00:40:14.671 --> 00:40:16.650
So, in our example, there
would be two groups,

00:40:16.651 --> 00:40:19.390
but you could be more general.

00:40:19.391 --> 00:40:21.370
They're worried about the fact

00:40:21.371 --> 00:40:22.630
that you may have a lack of

00:40:22.631 --> 00:40:26.605
training data for
your minority group.

00:40:26.606 --> 00:40:28.200
So, for the sage leader,

00:40:28.201 --> 00:40:30.510
you don't have a lot
of training data.

00:40:30.511 --> 00:40:35.805
What they require is
that at each round,

00:40:35.806 --> 00:40:38.150
let's say, think about
this in terms of loan.

00:40:38.151 --> 00:40:39.830
At each round, one person

00:40:39.831 --> 00:40:41.640
from each demographic group comes

00:40:41.641 --> 00:40:46.650
and you have to choose
one to give a loan to,

00:40:46.651 --> 00:40:49.265
or at most one to give a loan to.

00:40:49.266 --> 00:40:51.450
Maybe what you'll do is
you'll actually choose

00:40:51.451 --> 00:40:53.440
a probability
distribution and you will

00:40:53.441 --> 00:40:56.550
then choose somebody
from that distribution.

00:40:56.551 --> 00:41:00.995
The requirement is that
a worse applicant will never be

00:41:00.996 --> 00:41:03.295
favored in terms of
these probabilities

00:41:03.296 --> 00:41:06.110
over a better one
despite uncertainty.

00:41:06.111 --> 00:41:09.970
So, they study how to minimize

00:41:09.971 --> 00:41:14.230
the regret and learn
a fair policy.

00:41:14.231 --> 00:41:17.190
So, they look at
the difference between

00:41:17.191 --> 00:41:18.360
the regret that you get when you

00:41:18.361 --> 00:41:20.670
enforce this kind of
fairness condition,

00:41:20.671 --> 00:41:23.110
which basically costs you while

00:41:23.111 --> 00:41:25.405
you were learning about
the minority group.

00:41:25.406 --> 00:41:27.390
Once you know
the minority group well,

00:41:27.391 --> 00:41:28.910
you can predict with confidence,

00:41:28.911 --> 00:41:30.885
but when you don't, can't.

00:41:30.886 --> 00:41:32.530
So, they show that there's a gap,

00:41:32.531 --> 00:41:34.005
that it's more expensive.

00:41:34.006 --> 00:41:36.170
You have higher regret
when you're learning

00:41:36.171 --> 00:41:38.200
a fair policy versus regret when

00:41:38.201 --> 00:41:40.345
fairness is not a concern.
There was a question?

00:41:40.346 --> 00:41:43.140
&gt;&gt; Yes. So, what is the fairness
that are used here?

00:41:43.141 --> 00:41:47.495
&gt;&gt; They're using individual
fairness at each round.

00:41:47.496 --> 00:41:50.620
Among the people who have
shown up at that round.

00:41:50.621 --> 00:41:52.220
&gt;&gt; The probability that you

00:41:52.221 --> 00:41:54.350
choose usually shouldn't differ.

00:41:54.351 --> 00:41:56.300
&gt;&gt; Right, shouldn't be.

00:41:56.301 --> 00:41:58.470
Actually, no sorry.
There's something

