00:43:10.831 --> 00:43:13.920
the intersectional case and they

00:43:13.921 --> 00:43:18.965
want to learn a classifier
that will essentially ensure,

00:43:18.966 --> 00:43:21.090
well they looked at
a few different notions,

00:43:21.091 --> 00:43:22.525
statistical parody,

00:43:22.526 --> 00:43:25.705
and equalizing the false
positive or false negative rates

00:43:25.706 --> 00:43:29.830
across these intersectional sets.

00:43:30.490 --> 00:43:35.740
So, I described it as being
defined by say K bits,

00:43:35.741 --> 00:43:37.505
all of the subsets
to those K bits.

00:43:37.506 --> 00:43:38.945
But you can have other ways of

00:43:38.946 --> 00:43:41.145
describing your
collection of sets,

00:43:41.146 --> 00:43:43.260
like a set of all the sets

00:43:43.261 --> 00:43:45.650
that can be described
by small circuits.

00:43:45.651 --> 00:43:50.640
So, as long as the set is
these sets are large enough,

00:43:50.641 --> 00:43:53.040
they want to try to guarantee

00:43:53.041 --> 00:43:56.180
these notions of fairness
across the groups.

00:43:56.181 --> 00:43:58.400
They also showed that

00:43:58.401 --> 00:44:00.330
the general problem
of what they call

00:44:00.331 --> 00:44:02.550
fairness auditing is hard.

00:44:02.551 --> 00:44:03.960
So I give you a classifier and

00:44:03.961 --> 00:44:07.170
these descriptions and perhaps
implicit of these groups,

00:44:07.171 --> 00:44:10.310
how do you determine
whether there

00:44:10.311 --> 00:44:13.690
is a group to whom
you're being unfair?

00:44:13.691 --> 00:44:15.610
When one of these
intersectional things

00:44:15.611 --> 00:44:17.215
to whom you're being unfair,

00:44:17.216 --> 00:44:19.010
and they showed hardness by

00:44:19.011 --> 00:44:21.265
reduction from agnostic learning.

00:44:21.266 --> 00:44:23.800
So, the reason they
can have an algorithm

00:44:23.801 --> 00:44:26.330
as well as these negative
results is as you all

00:44:26.331 --> 00:44:27.800
know machine learning often does

00:44:27.801 --> 00:44:32.045
interesting things even when
in theory they're hard.

00:44:32.046 --> 00:44:37.020
Very similar questions were
looked at by Hebert-Johnson,

00:44:37.021 --> 00:44:38.530
Kim, Reingold and Roth.

00:44:38.531 --> 00:44:43.810
Wait I made a mistake
this is Roth Blom.

00:44:43.811 --> 00:44:46.605
The other one is Roth
this is Roth Blom.

00:44:46.606 --> 00:44:49.395
They studied calibration.

00:44:49.396 --> 00:44:57.840
So suppose my predictor
is now giving a score,

00:44:57.841 --> 00:44:59.800
and so I'm going to rate people

00:44:59.801 --> 00:45:01.610
some number between zero and one.

00:45:01.611 --> 00:45:03.860
Think about this as
my prediction about

00:45:03.861 --> 00:45:06.595
your probability of
recidivating, for example.

00:45:06.596 --> 00:45:09.025
So, the system is calibrated

00:45:09.026 --> 00:45:11.330
if among those that are rated v,

00:45:11.331 --> 00:45:13.770
the fraction who truly
will end up with

00:45:13.771 --> 00:45:16.640
a positive label
is close to v. So,

00:45:16.641 --> 00:45:19.670
in other words it's requiring
correctness on average for

00:45:19.671 --> 00:45:23.195
those rated v for all v. So,

00:45:23.196 --> 00:45:25.340
they got results that
were very similar

00:45:25.341 --> 00:45:27.790
but they're using
this kind of notion

00:45:27.791 --> 00:45:30.975
of correctness as fairness

00:45:30.976 --> 00:45:33.305
through the calibration version.

00:45:33.306 --> 00:45:35.090
If you're familiar
with the Kleinberg,

00:45:35.091 --> 00:45:37.325
Mullainathan, and
Raghavan result,

00:45:37.326 --> 00:45:40.450
this is the kind of
calibration notion

00:45:40.451 --> 00:45:43.725
there or related
calibration notion.

00:45:43.726 --> 00:45:45.750
They argue that this makes more

00:45:45.751 --> 00:45:48.130
sense than these other
group fairness notions

00:45:48.131 --> 00:45:52.170
but other than that they
get very similar results.

00:45:52.171 --> 00:45:55.180
So, for every set

00:45:55.181 --> 00:45:58.260
defined by a circuit in
a predetermined set C,

00:45:58.261 --> 00:46:02.980
they get this approximate
calibration simultaneously.

00:46:03.140 --> 00:46:09.870
Subsequent work by Michael Kim
and James Zoe has shown

00:46:09.871 --> 00:46:11.880
that they've used this to

00:46:11.881 --> 00:46:17.125
improve some medical predictors.

00:46:17.126 --> 00:46:20.010
They've gotten some nice results.

00:46:20.310 --> 00:46:23.500
Then, how long is my talk?

00:46:23.501 --> 00:46:31.900
&gt;&gt; [inaudible].

00:46:31.901 --> 00:46:34.070
&gt;&gt; Okay, I'll talk for
just a little more then.

00:46:34.071 --> 00:46:38.230
Okay, so, the next batch
of results deals

00:46:38.231 --> 00:46:40.310
with algorithmic work
that tries to

00:46:40.311 --> 00:46:42.130
bridge the gap between

00:46:42.131 --> 00:46:44.580
group fairness and
individual fairness

00:46:44.581 --> 00:46:45.650
by assuming that we have

00:46:45.651 --> 00:46:49.270
some sort of oracle that tells us

00:46:49.640 --> 00:46:53.380
for we can ask

00:46:53.381 --> 00:46:56.150
about certain collections
of edges and say,

00:46:56.151 --> 00:46:58.155
"What are the distances
on these edges?"

00:46:58.156 --> 00:47:00.655
And then it leverages that to do

00:47:00.656 --> 00:47:02.470
powerful things without requiring

00:47:02.471 --> 00:47:03.730
you to ask about all pairs.

00:47:03.731 --> 00:47:07.550
In the deep learning community,

00:47:07.551 --> 00:47:09.530
there's a whole different
approach which is

00:47:09.531 --> 00:47:11.670
adversarial learning or learning

00:47:11.671 --> 00:47:16.120
a fair representations and
adversarial version of that.

00:47:16.121 --> 00:47:20.750
So, this is a group
fairness notion. Oh shoot.

00:47:20.751 --> 00:47:26.365
Sorry, all right let
me skip this slide.

00:47:26.366 --> 00:47:32.540
Okay, so the general idea

00:47:32.541 --> 00:47:36.130
here is we start with
instances in some set X

00:47:36.131 --> 00:47:39.645
and we're going to learn

00:47:39.646 --> 00:47:41.630
a new representation for

00:47:41.631 --> 00:47:45.090
people that in some sense retains

00:47:45.091 --> 00:47:48.990
as much information as
possible while suppressing

00:47:48.991 --> 00:47:51.350
some key attributes such
as whether somebody is

00:47:51.351 --> 00:47:53.850
or is not a member
of a sage eater,

00:47:53.851 --> 00:47:56.115
whether they're saying
eater or a time eater.

00:47:56.116 --> 00:48:03.190
And they're using
gens and so we've got

00:48:03.191 --> 00:48:04.485
an adversary that is

00:48:04.486 --> 00:48:07.210
trying to distinguish
given a z whether

00:48:07.211 --> 00:48:12.410
it came from the sage eater X
or a time eater X.

00:48:12.650 --> 00:48:16.230
The key point in this intuition

00:48:16.231 --> 00:48:18.600
is the predictor is going

00:48:18.601 --> 00:48:21.245
to operate on
the representation Z.

00:48:21.246 --> 00:48:24.970
If the predictor is

00:48:25.500 --> 00:48:30.910
not so powerful as
the adversary who is trying

00:48:30.911 --> 00:48:32.640
to distinguish sage eaters from

00:48:32.641 --> 00:48:34.840
time eaters and who has trained

00:48:34.841 --> 00:48:36.360
the encoder to make

00:48:36.361 --> 00:48:40.780
that representation not
yield distinguishability,

00:48:40.781 --> 00:48:43.480
the predictor is no more
powerful than A than in

00:48:43.481 --> 00:48:47.520
some sense its predictions
can't be biased.

00:48:47.521 --> 00:48:50.650
This is really,
really intriguing.

00:48:50.651 --> 00:48:53.140
It is a group notion
of fairness and I

00:48:53.141 --> 00:48:55.410
would love to see
individual fairness

00:48:55.411 --> 00:48:58.210
worked in here but

00:48:58.750 --> 00:49:03.455
this is another very interesting
direction of research.

00:49:03.456 --> 00:49:06.480
So, in the last five minutes

00:49:06.481 --> 00:49:09.070
I'm going to talk
about composition.

00:49:09.220 --> 00:49:13.030
Because this is really
interesting and this is new.

00:49:13.031 --> 00:49:15.085
So, our intuition is,

00:49:15.086 --> 00:49:19.640
if we have a system in
which we have a bunch of

00:49:19.641 --> 00:49:22.180
individually fair
pieces then when

00:49:22.181 --> 00:49:25.005
we look at the system that's
made of these as a whole,

00:49:25.006 --> 00:49:27.385
it's still at least
relatively fair.

00:49:27.386 --> 00:49:31.580
And the answer is,
it's complicated.

00:49:31.581 --> 00:49:36.305
So, here's a very simple example.

00:49:36.306 --> 00:49:39.490
Suppose let's make the example

00:49:39.491 --> 00:49:40.840
really simple you're looking at

00:49:40.841 --> 00:49:42.940
the New York Times
website and there is

00:49:42.941 --> 00:49:46.895
a single slot for a banner ad
at the top of the page.

00:49:46.896 --> 00:49:50.110
That's going to be our scenario.

00:49:51.350 --> 00:49:55.270
As you know, there's an auction
run for your attention.

00:49:55.271 --> 00:49:59.280
Advertisers are competing
to get that slot.

00:49:59.810 --> 00:50:02.930
So, let's look at the case where

00:50:02.931 --> 00:50:05.170
we have two kinds of advertisers.

00:50:05.171 --> 00:50:08.200
One is advertising tech jobs and

00:50:08.201 --> 00:50:13.420
the other one is advertising
a grocery delivery service.

00:50:13.421 --> 00:50:15.830
So, the tech jobs advertiser and

00:50:15.831 --> 00:50:17.810
the grocery delivery
service advertiser

00:50:17.811 --> 00:50:20.460
are competing for your attention.

00:50:21.870 --> 00:50:26.690
Ideally if our classifier
for determining who is

00:50:26.691 --> 00:50:30.665
a a good bet for the tech
jobs ad is fair,

00:50:30.666 --> 00:50:33.230
and if our classifier
for determining who's

00:50:33.231 --> 00:50:36.510
a good bet for
the grocery ad is fair,

00:50:36.511 --> 00:50:39.665
then somehow the system
will be fair as a whole.

00:50:39.666 --> 00:50:44.535
But we want to require that
in this complex system,

00:50:44.536 --> 00:50:46.220
it's still the case
that people who are

00:50:46.221 --> 00:50:48.570
similarly qualified for tech jobs

00:50:48.571 --> 00:50:50.290
should be similarly likely to see

00:50:50.291 --> 00:50:53.665
tech job ads and same
thing for groceries.

00:50:53.666 --> 00:50:59.355
So, suppose that
the grocery advertiser

00:50:59.356 --> 00:51:04.255
routinely outbids
the tech jobs advertiser.

00:51:04.256 --> 00:51:08.050
It's an auction,
the outbidder wins.

00:51:08.051 --> 00:51:09.490
So we can even think of it as

00:51:09.491 --> 00:51:11.410
just the outbidder goes first.

00:51:11.411 --> 00:51:13.910
The grocery advertiser
is going first or

00:51:13.911 --> 00:51:17.310
its bidding more, all right?

00:51:17.311 --> 00:51:20.440
And so the tech company is

00:51:20.441 --> 00:51:23.510
now essentially being
reduced to the leftovers,

00:51:23.511 --> 00:51:27.285
the ones who weren't grabbed
by the groceries advertiser.

00:51:27.286 --> 00:51:30.560
This is true again whether
it's done temporally

00:51:30.561 --> 00:51:32.080
first one than the other

00:51:32.081 --> 00:51:34.100
or whether it's just
done by the money bids.

00:51:34.101 --> 00:51:40.565
So, this is a major problem.

00:51:40.566 --> 00:51:42.895
It says that no matter

00:51:42.896 --> 00:51:46.740
how qualified you are
for a tech jobs ad,

00:51:46.741 --> 00:51:50.370
if you're very qualified
for groceries,

00:51:50.371 --> 00:51:53.185
you're not going to
see the tech jobs ads.

00:51:53.186 --> 00:51:59.770
Now who is qualified for
the grocery service,

00:51:59.771 --> 00:52:01.890
the grocery delivery service?

00:52:01.891 --> 00:52:04.345
New parents.

00:52:04.346 --> 00:52:07.840
Advertisers drool over the things

00:52:07.841 --> 00:52:09.359
they can start advertising

00:52:09.360 --> 00:52:11.410
to new parents and those tend

00:52:11.411 --> 00:52:13.840
not to be the tech
jobs advertisers,

00:52:13.841 --> 00:52:16.620
and in fact, the tech jobs
advertisers you might

00:52:16.621 --> 00:52:19.380
say shouldn't touch
the parenthood bid

00:52:19.381 --> 00:52:21.195
with a 10 foot pole.

00:52:21.196 --> 00:52:23.290
So, even if they are really

00:52:23.291 --> 00:52:27.225
appropriately not looking
at parenthood status,

00:52:27.226 --> 00:52:29.700
you would think it was
appropriate not to be

00:52:29.701 --> 00:52:31.940
looking at parenthood status
in determining

00:52:31.941 --> 00:52:34.520
who should see the ad there're

00:52:34.521 --> 00:52:36.695
competitors for your attention

00:52:36.696 --> 00:52:39.010
are looking at that
and that's affecting

00:52:39.011 --> 00:52:42.340
what happens under
the tech jobs advertiser.

00:52:42.341 --> 00:52:46.170
Okay and there are versions of

00:52:46.171 --> 00:52:47.925
this particular problem both for

00:52:47.926 --> 00:52:50.725
individual fairness and
for group fairness.

00:52:50.726 --> 00:52:55.080
So, this problem doesn't go away.

00:52:55.081 --> 00:52:57.580
No time for these things.

00:52:57.730 --> 00:53:01.600
There is a way out here though
which I will mention which

00:53:01.601 --> 00:53:04.420
is we could fix

00:53:04.421 --> 00:53:07.705
a probability distribution
on the advertising tasks.

00:53:07.706 --> 00:53:09.945
Any probability
distribution we want.

00:53:09.946 --> 00:53:13.100
Then we pick a task according
to that distribution,

00:53:13.101 --> 00:53:15.280
and then we run
the classifier just

00:53:15.281 --> 00:53:17.360
for the chosen task and

00:53:17.361 --> 00:53:18.550
the proof that this gives

00:53:18.551 --> 00:53:22.855
us individual fairness
is very, very simple.

00:53:22.856 --> 00:53:26.050
But it leaves money on
the table because there are

00:53:26.051 --> 00:53:27.360
some people for whom we are

00:53:27.361 --> 00:53:29.715
just not going to be showing ads.

00:53:29.716 --> 00:53:31.900
So, that raises a number of

00:53:31.901 --> 00:53:33.840
very interesting
economics questions

00:53:33.841 --> 00:53:34.940
for those who are interested in

00:53:34.941 --> 00:53:38.480
the intersection of
algorithms and economics.

00:53:38.481 --> 00:53:45.040
Because of time, I
will skip the rest.

00:53:45.840 --> 00:53:48.850
&gt;&gt; Final comments.

00:53:48.851 --> 00:53:53.360
First of all, Interpretability
and Causality.

00:53:53.361 --> 00:53:56.375
There are big literatures

00:53:56.376 --> 00:54:01.035
on interpretability and
causality for fairness.

00:54:01.036 --> 00:54:04.235
Why is this slide left blank?

00:54:04.236 --> 00:54:07.665
First of all, I have
tried very hard

00:54:07.666 --> 00:54:14.635
to make headway with
interpretability,

00:54:14.636 --> 00:54:16.535
and I have failed.

00:54:16.536 --> 00:54:21.125
I don't understand
what's being asked,

00:54:21.126 --> 00:54:23.780
and how it can be achieved.

00:54:23.781 --> 00:54:25.990
There's a very interesting
article called

00:54:25.991 --> 00:54:29.435
the Mythos of Interpretability
by Zachary Lipton.

00:54:29.436 --> 00:54:32.365
He explains, he catalogs,

00:54:32.366 --> 00:54:34.310
these are some things
that people could want,

00:54:34.311 --> 00:54:35.815
and here are some of the issues.

00:54:35.816 --> 00:54:37.760
It's very, very thoughtful,

00:54:37.761 --> 00:54:39.470
and I'm not done
thinking about this,

00:54:39.471 --> 00:54:42.635
but I'm stumped on this one.

00:54:42.636 --> 00:54:46.425
As far as causality,

00:54:46.426 --> 00:54:50.510
it's problematic for a number
of reasons that I can see.

00:54:50.511 --> 00:54:53.390
So, first of all,
if you're using,

00:54:53.391 --> 00:54:55.515
say Pearl's theory
of causal reasoning,

00:54:55.516 --> 00:54:57.360
you need a generative model.

00:54:57.361 --> 00:54:59.240
Now, maybe you're familiar with

00:54:59.241 --> 00:55:01.380
the saying that all
models are wrong,

00:55:01.381 --> 00:55:03.185
but some models are useful.

00:55:03.186 --> 00:55:05.440
So, if your definition of

00:55:05.441 --> 00:55:08.955
fairness ties the
classifier to the model,

00:55:08.956 --> 00:55:10.560
and you need those two

00:55:10.561 --> 00:55:12.950
together to determine
if something is fair,

00:55:12.951 --> 00:55:14.680
and your model is wrong,

00:55:14.681 --> 00:55:17.535
you may very well come up
with the wrong conclusion.

00:55:17.536 --> 00:55:19.770
So, that's a glimpse into

00:55:19.771 --> 00:55:21.540
what some of
the difficulties I've

00:55:21.541 --> 00:55:26.070
encountered with causality are.

00:55:28.810 --> 00:55:34.480
Another brief comment as we've
talked about the metric,

00:55:34.481 --> 00:55:35.640
we've talked about the issues

00:55:35.641 --> 00:55:36.770
of where do you get the metric

00:55:36.771 --> 00:55:44.750
from, there's still problems.

00:55:44.751 --> 00:55:53.370
So, here, suppose we have
news organization, a nice news.

00:55:55.500 --> 00:55:59.395
Your news organization
has an environment,

00:55:59.396 --> 00:56:01.930
there's randomness
in the environment,

00:56:01.931 --> 00:56:03.760
you have an individual x,

00:56:03.761 --> 00:56:05.660
there's randomness in x,

00:56:05.661 --> 00:56:07.470
but you can talk about,

00:56:07.471 --> 00:56:09.600
although you can't
get your hands on,

00:56:09.601 --> 00:56:13.910
the probability that
this individual x

00:56:13.911 --> 00:56:18.335
will succeed at nice news.

00:56:18.336 --> 00:56:21.130
It's well-defined
once you specify

00:56:21.131 --> 00:56:22.850
the coins for this randomness and

00:56:22.851 --> 00:56:24.485
the coins for this randomness.

00:56:24.486 --> 00:56:25.990
Again, you can't get
your hands on it,

00:56:25.991 --> 00:56:28.300
but at least it makes
sense mathematically.

00:56:28.301 --> 00:56:30.745
Now, what you might require

00:56:30.746 --> 00:56:33.395
is that if two people
are similar,

00:56:33.396 --> 00:56:37.255
meaning they have similar
probabilities now in success.

00:56:37.256 --> 00:56:39.270
And success can be
very crisply defined,

00:56:39.271 --> 00:56:41.680
like you stay in the company
for at least three years,

00:56:41.681 --> 00:56:43.350
and you have
at least one promotion

00:56:43.351 --> 00:56:45.880
during that time, okay?

00:56:46.160 --> 00:56:49.140
You might say that if you
have two individuals who

00:56:49.141 --> 00:56:51.130
are so equally likely to succeed,

00:56:51.131 --> 00:56:52.360
then they should
have very similar

00:56:52.361 --> 00:56:55.420
probabilities of being hired.

00:56:56.200 --> 00:56:58.790
That seems to make some sense,

00:56:58.791 --> 00:56:59.950
so the metric then is

00:56:59.951 --> 00:57:03.020
capturing the
probability of success.

00:57:03.840 --> 00:57:07.250
But, what happens if
this is not nice news,

00:57:07.251 --> 00:57:08.350
but it's nasty news,

00:57:08.351 --> 00:57:12.000
which has a history of being
very hostile to sage eaters.

00:57:12.460 --> 00:57:14.760
So, sage eaters can't

00:57:14.761 --> 00:57:17.490
succeed no matter how
talented they are,

00:57:17.491 --> 00:57:21.870
or they'll have
a real uphill struggle.

00:57:22.000 --> 00:57:24.565
Should the metric, in fact,

00:57:24.566 --> 00:57:26.200
be capturing whether people

00:57:26.201 --> 00:57:28.640
are equally likely to succeed?

00:57:28.641 --> 00:57:32.200
Should it be capturing whether
they're equally talented?

00:57:32.201 --> 00:57:35.030
What's the right
thing to do here?

00:57:35.031 --> 00:57:38.900
It's not clear.
So, final remarks,

00:57:38.901 --> 00:57:41.600
truth is elusive,
it's not remedy.

00:57:41.601 --> 00:57:46.200
This problem is not remedied
by having a computer.

00:57:52.840 --> 00:57:55.440
They've argued, I really believed

00:57:55.441 --> 00:57:57.240
that metrics are really at

00:57:57.241 --> 00:57:59.035
the heart of everything even if

00:57:59.036 --> 00:58:01.380
it may take us
a long time to find them,

00:58:01.381 --> 00:58:04.620
but we advocate always
sunshine for the metric.

00:58:04.621 --> 00:58:06.360
The metric should
not be kept secret,

00:58:06.361 --> 00:58:07.630
it has to be open for

00:58:07.631 --> 00:58:10.880
debate and discussion
and refinement.

00:58:10.881 --> 00:58:15.080
Computers are not necessarily
worse than humans,

00:58:15.081 --> 00:58:17.210
they may be more accurate,

00:58:17.211 --> 00:58:19.390
especially on the easy cases,

00:58:19.391 --> 00:58:21.540
they may be more easily tested,

00:58:21.541 --> 00:58:23.650
they don't get tired
when you keep throwing

00:58:23.651 --> 00:58:26.770
examples at them to see
what they have to do.

00:58:27.340 --> 00:58:30.360
That's it. Thank you.

00:58:38.320 --> 00:58:39.840
&gt;&gt; There's a question.

00:58:39.841 --> 00:58:41.605
&gt;&gt; Sure. Go ahead.

00:58:41.606 --> 00:58:44.185
Sorry, one behind, and then you.

00:58:44.186 --> 00:58:48.490
&gt;&gt; So, I'm really
[inaudible] a lot.

00:58:48.491 --> 00:58:50.170
I'm sure you thought
about this and might

00:58:50.171 --> 00:58:52.810
even mentioned it in
the previous slide,

00:58:52.811 --> 00:58:54.295
when you have curious
figure of two thing,

00:58:54.296 --> 00:58:58.630
so in considering
the individual there,

00:58:58.631 --> 00:59:01.645
if we're looking at time eaters,

00:59:01.646 --> 00:59:03.450
time eaters always
teach their kids,

00:59:03.451 --> 00:59:10.440
and there is this terrible
sage brush infection,

00:59:10.650 --> 00:59:13.745
anyone who eats
sage get very ill,

00:59:13.746 --> 00:59:17.100
also there is this terrible
historical injustice

00:59:17.101 --> 00:59:18.270
right off the sage eaters.

00:59:18.271 --> 00:59:21.585
They just did weigh less
good, apparently everything,

00:59:21.586 --> 00:59:23.915
they're so focused on
handling this illness.

00:59:23.916 --> 00:59:25.430
So, it just don't happens

00:59:25.431 --> 00:59:27.585
via this historical coincidence,

00:59:27.586 --> 00:59:29.570
the sage eaters are almost never

00:59:29.571 --> 00:59:31.960
like a similarly
situated individual,

00:59:31.961 --> 00:59:33.880
they compete with
the time eaters.

00:59:33.881 --> 00:59:36.935
It seems like on
that individual fairness metric,

00:59:36.936 --> 00:59:38.975
we would just say,
okay no problem,

00:59:38.976 --> 00:59:40.350
and if we're designing jobs,

00:59:40.351 --> 00:59:42.460
and it's the time eaters
they get all of them,

00:59:42.461 --> 00:59:44.080
whereas we're not
considering metric

00:59:44.081 --> 00:59:45.800
of group aspect and I think

00:59:45.801 --> 00:59:49.740
somewhere along [inaudible] societies
[inaudible] historical justice.

00:59:49.741 --> 00:59:52.150
&gt;&gt; That's, of course,
an excellent point,

00:59:52.151 --> 00:59:53.730
and in the paper,

00:59:53.731 --> 00:59:57.460
we described what we call
Fair Affirmative Action,

00:59:57.461 --> 01:00:02.420
which is a method for
dealing with this,

01:00:02.421 --> 01:00:07.040
where we break certain

01:00:07.140 --> 01:00:10.850
of the Lipschitz constraints
for exactly this reason,

01:00:10.851 --> 01:00:14.560
but it's a principled
approach to this thing.

01:00:14.561 --> 01:00:18.045
I should mention that it's

01:00:18.046 --> 01:00:22.840
not so unlike what the state
of California does,

01:00:22.841 --> 01:00:27.585
and the state of Texas does
with respect to college.

01:00:27.586 --> 01:00:31.785
That if you're in the top
10 percent of your class,

01:00:31.786 --> 01:00:40.640
then you're admitted to one
of the state in California,

01:00:40.641 --> 01:00:43.440
it's one of the UCs,
and in Texas,

01:00:43.441 --> 01:00:47.510
it's one of the state
colleges, I guess.

01:00:48.870 --> 01:00:51.470
The point is that schools in

01:00:51.471 --> 01:00:53.930
different neighborhoods
may have very,

01:00:53.931 --> 01:00:56.700
very different
performances on tests,

01:00:56.701 --> 01:00:59.980
and so on, and so forth, but
it's the same basic idea.

01:00:59.981 --> 01:01:02.410
So, we add a bit to that,

01:01:02.411 --> 01:01:05.445
it's not quite as course as
taking the top 10 percent.

01:01:05.446 --> 01:01:13.840
There's a very interesting work
by John Roemer at Yale,

01:01:13.841 --> 01:01:16.150
who is very concerned

01:01:16.151 --> 01:01:18.865
with theories of
distributive justice,

01:01:18.866 --> 01:01:22.390
and he thinks a lot
about education.

01:01:22.391 --> 01:01:27.865
He thinks that you must
invest, and invest,

01:01:27.866 --> 01:01:31.530
and invest in children
in order to compensate

01:01:31.531 --> 01:01:37.155
for differences of situation,

01:01:37.156 --> 01:01:43.050
and that as you go through
the schooling process,

01:01:43.051 --> 01:01:45.640
you do less and less of
this compensating because

01:01:45.641 --> 01:01:47.970
the children having been given

01:01:47.971 --> 01:01:50.805
a good foundation or more,
theoretically, I guess,

01:01:50.806 --> 01:01:56.735
more responsible for how they
make use of what they have,

01:01:56.736 --> 01:02:01.530
but in particular, I remember

01:02:01.531 --> 01:02:03.300
he said the following

01:02:03.301 --> 01:02:06.655
in the context of
college admissions.

01:02:06.656 --> 01:02:09.080
Stratify the students according

01:02:09.081 --> 01:02:11.935
to the education level
of the mother,

01:02:11.936 --> 01:02:15.040
and within each stratum,

01:02:15.041 --> 01:02:18.245
look at the cumulative
distribution function

01:02:18.246 --> 01:02:20.315
describing how many hours a

01:02:20.316 --> 01:02:22.745
week the students
spend on homework,

01:02:22.746 --> 01:02:26.510
and view people in
the various percentiles in

01:02:26.511 --> 01:02:31.135
that CDF from the different
strata, as being comparable.

01:02:31.136 --> 01:02:33.770
One of the points he makes,

01:02:33.771 --> 01:02:37.165
which I found extremely
compelling is that,

01:02:37.166 --> 01:02:43.995
if you're brought up in a home
where nobody is educated,

01:02:43.996 --> 01:02:46.710
then you may not
even think that it's

01:02:46.711 --> 01:02:49.935
possible to spend 10 or 15 hours
a week on homework.

01:02:49.936 --> 01:02:52.155
It just doesn't cross
your mind to do it,

01:02:52.156 --> 01:02:53.480
or you might be working,

01:02:53.481 --> 01:02:55.820
and unable to do it.

01:02:56.340 --> 01:02:58.785
I think that these sorts of

01:02:58.786 --> 01:03:02.420
insights from the social
scientists are very important

01:03:02.421 --> 01:03:05.270
in what we are doing
is in some sense

01:03:05.271 --> 01:03:09.430
analogous to that. It's
a very good point.

01:03:10.320 --> 01:03:13.090
&gt;&gt; So, that whole conversation is

01:03:13.091 --> 01:03:15.670
basically related
to this nasty news,

01:03:15.671 --> 01:03:17.640
choosing the metric thing,

01:03:17.641 --> 01:03:20.490
and it comes down to questions of

01:03:20.491 --> 01:03:25.000
what the condition on
mediating variables and stuff.

01:03:25.001 --> 01:03:27.410
So, basically, the rest of

01:03:27.411 --> 01:03:30.500
the talk didn't address
those types of issues,

01:03:30.501 --> 01:03:33.360
like how to choose
the metric fairly.

01:03:33.361 --> 01:03:36.580
Is there any work that uses

01:03:36.581 --> 01:03:40.100
graphical models or other ideas
for causal inference to,

01:03:40.101 --> 01:03:42.325
because we have
this intuitive idea, oh,

01:03:42.326 --> 01:03:43.750
well we should condition on

01:03:43.751 --> 01:03:45.730
these things we want
to make a fair metric,

01:03:45.731 --> 01:03:46.720
but we definitely don't want to

01:03:46.721 --> 01:03:48.480
condition on these other things.

01:03:48.481 --> 01:03:50.925
Is there any work that
tries to formalize that?

01:03:50.926 --> 01:03:53.020
&gt;&gt; There is work.

01:04:00.490 --> 01:04:02.725
It's very interesting,

01:04:02.726 --> 01:04:03.925
and if you're interested,

01:04:03.926 --> 01:04:05.995
I can point you to
a bunch of papers.

01:04:05.996 --> 01:04:10.780
The one difficulty with some
of this work is that you can

01:04:10.781 --> 01:04:16.120
have the same
observable distribution

01:04:16.121 --> 01:04:17.700
on data that's consistent

01:04:17.701 --> 01:04:19.975
with very different
causal models,

01:04:19.976 --> 01:04:25.300
and so that just causes problems.

01:04:25.301 --> 01:04:28.680
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] models
is just really hard.

01:04:30.870 --> 01:04:35.440
&gt;&gt; So, I don't know what
to say about that yet.

01:04:35.441 --> 01:04:37.520
I was really excited when

01:04:37.521 --> 01:04:40.180
I saw that there was
a counterfactual fairness paper.

01:04:40.181 --> 01:04:41.400
I thought, "Yes,
this is it, this is

01:04:41.401 --> 01:04:42.880
it," but then we found

01:04:42.881 --> 01:04:44.320
two different models
that gave rise

01:04:44.321 --> 01:04:46.265
to the same distribution,

01:04:46.266 --> 01:04:48.800
and the classifier, and the
thing was fair under one,

01:04:48.801 --> 01:04:51.400
and not under the other.
So, what do you do?

01:04:51.960 --> 01:04:56.470
&gt;&gt; Yes.

01:04:56.471 --> 01:04:59.075
&gt;&gt; Suppose I want to get
a chess team for my school.

01:04:59.076 --> 01:04:59.680
&gt;&gt; You want to get what?

01:04:59.681 --> 01:05:01.180
&gt;&gt; I'm sorry, select
the chess team of

01:05:01.181 --> 01:05:04.100
three people to represent
my school at some company.

01:05:04.101 --> 01:05:06.350
What I'd probably do
to try and measure

01:05:06.351 --> 01:05:07.910
how good the people are and pick

01:05:07.911 --> 01:05:11.720
the best three by measurement,

01:05:11.721 --> 01:05:13.420
and see if they fit
your definition

01:05:13.421 --> 01:05:15.300
of individual fairness.

01:05:15.301 --> 01:05:15.560
&gt;&gt; Yes.

01:05:15.561 --> 01:05:17.920
&gt;&gt; The more accurately
I can measure people,

01:05:17.921 --> 01:05:19.195
the less randomness there is,

01:05:19.196 --> 01:05:21.060
and the less fair it is.

01:05:21.061 --> 01:05:23.240
&gt;&gt; You introduced the randomness.

01:05:23.241 --> 01:05:24.565
&gt;&gt; There is some random.

01:05:24.566 --> 01:05:27.570
&gt;&gt; You say that the algorithm
introduces the randomness.

01:05:27.571 --> 01:05:30.790
&gt;&gt; I mean, if you just take
what people typically do,

01:05:30.791 --> 01:05:32.010
they're not going to introduce

01:05:32.011 --> 01:05:33.410
randomness out of the fact
but there will be

01:05:33.411 --> 01:05:36.360
randomness and because some
people win, some people lose.

01:05:36.361 --> 01:05:37.490
There's randomness
in how well you

01:05:37.491 --> 01:05:40.520
can measure people's skill.

01:05:40.700 --> 01:05:43.745
It seemed like by
your definition,

01:05:43.746 --> 01:05:47.980
the more accurately we can
measure people's skill,

01:05:47.981 --> 01:05:49.580
the less randomness there is,

01:05:49.581 --> 01:05:53.110
the less fair
the selection process.

01:05:53.111 --> 01:05:57.600
&gt;&gt; If you're saying that you
can get very, very, very.

01:05:58.210 --> 01:06:01.270
&gt;&gt; Maybe, wait a minute, Y is

01:06:01.271 --> 01:06:03.685
a completely random process

01:06:03.686 --> 01:06:06.120
more fair than a meritocratic Y.

01:06:06.121 --> 01:06:08.025
&gt;&gt; Right. Okay. This is

01:06:08.026 --> 01:06:09.460
the kind of problem
that shows up,

01:06:09.461 --> 01:06:11.570
in fairness, all the time.

01:06:11.571 --> 01:06:17.560
I mean, from
this particular example,

01:06:17.561 --> 01:06:19.675
you'd hit the nail on the head.

01:06:19.676 --> 01:06:23.375
In the definition of
individual fairness,

01:06:23.376 --> 01:06:26.650
buried in there is the idea
that if you simply flipped

01:06:26.651 --> 01:06:29.925
coins all the time,
that would be fair.

01:06:29.926 --> 01:06:32.010
If all you did was decide whether

01:06:32.011 --> 01:06:34.295
someone could be on the chess
team by flipping a coin,

01:06:34.296 --> 01:06:37.260
certainly similar people
would be treated similarly.

01:06:37.261 --> 01:06:39.310
What we're not ensuring is that

01:06:39.311 --> 01:06:42.055
dissimilar people are
treated dissimilarly.

01:06:42.056 --> 01:06:44.710
This is where we want to throw in

01:06:44.711 --> 01:06:47.240
they will minimize
the loss subject to

01:06:47.241 --> 01:06:49.160
the fairness conditions
and we want to

01:06:49.161 --> 01:06:53.120
incorporate some notion of
loss in the decision process.

01:06:53.480 --> 01:06:56.340
We want to stay fair but we also

01:06:56.341 --> 01:06:59.630
want to maximize utility.

01:07:00.210 --> 01:07:06.930
&gt;&gt; I think it's not
fair to be [inaudible]

01:07:06.931 --> 01:07:15.290
a fairness constraint has to
be random. That's simple.

01:07:22.380 --> 01:07:25.240
&gt;&gt; I know what you're saying,

01:07:25.241 --> 01:07:27.750
and you can probably come up with

01:07:27.751 --> 01:07:30.310
even better examples of where

01:07:30.311 --> 01:07:32.870
randomness is unfair or

01:07:32.871 --> 01:07:35.720
where treating everybody
the same way is unfair.

01:07:35.721 --> 01:07:40.060
Often, it's possible to

01:07:40.700 --> 01:07:44.270
reframe the problem so
that you're pushing those

01:07:44.271 --> 01:07:46.650
concerned into
the objective function

01:07:46.651 --> 01:07:48.730
rather than the fairness.

01:07:49.710 --> 01:07:52.600
Yes. By the way,

01:07:52.601 --> 01:07:54.570
we thought that we
were so original with

01:07:54.571 --> 01:07:56.705
similar treated
similarly but of course,

01:07:56.706 --> 01:07:58.345
Aristotle says the same thing,

01:07:58.346 --> 01:08:00.470
and Aristotle says dissimilar

01:08:00.471 --> 01:08:02.165
should be treated dissimilarly.

01:08:02.166 --> 01:08:04.910
Since we were thinking
initially about advertising,

01:08:04.911 --> 01:08:06.470
and I was thinking
about how when you look

01:08:06.471 --> 01:08:08.775
at a physical newspaper,

01:08:08.776 --> 01:08:11.810
everybody gets the same ads.

01:08:11.811 --> 01:08:14.145
There's no targeting.

01:08:14.146 --> 01:08:16.455
That seems pretty fair.

01:08:16.456 --> 01:08:19.880
In terms of exposing

01:08:19.881 --> 01:08:23.390
young people to ads
for luxury goods,

01:08:23.391 --> 01:08:26.180
does that help form
aspirations or not?

01:08:26.181 --> 01:08:28.030
Should you be saying
that poor people should

01:08:28.031 --> 01:08:29.970
not see poor children,

01:08:29.971 --> 01:08:33.730
shouldn't see nice things
or pictures of nice things?

01:08:33.731 --> 01:08:35.270
There are people who work

01:08:35.271 --> 01:08:37.190
on these kinds of questions from

01:08:37.191 --> 01:08:39.480
the psychological and
sociological point of

01:08:39.481 --> 01:08:42.530
view and they think that yes,

01:08:42.531 --> 01:08:44.850
the ads are very tied in

01:08:44.851 --> 01:08:46.925
with the formulation
of the self and that

01:08:46.926 --> 01:08:51.565
having a real difference in
what people see is harmful.

01:08:51.566 --> 01:08:57.600
So, I don't know. Yes.

01:08:57.601 --> 01:08:58.750
&gt;&gt; When hearing the conversation

01:08:58.751 --> 01:09:00.425
of individual fairness metrics,

01:09:00.426 --> 01:09:03.400
I was thinking about
drug treatment effects

01:09:03.401 --> 01:09:04.790
and where you're doing

01:09:04.791 --> 01:09:06.425
these randomized
controlled assignments.

01:09:06.426 --> 01:09:08.570
You're thinking about,
here's the population,

01:09:08.571 --> 01:09:09.790
let me find someone from

01:09:09.791 --> 01:09:11.965
this other population
that matters well,

01:09:11.966 --> 01:09:13.480
see the treatment effect

01:09:13.481 --> 01:09:16.025
when different treatments
are assigned.

01:09:16.026 --> 01:09:17.705
Then really, I can imagine

01:09:17.706 --> 01:09:20.860
a different sort of problem
here where you really want

01:09:20.861 --> 01:09:23.160
the classifier to behave
like a placebo for

01:09:23.161 --> 01:09:25.430
these matched individuals rather

01:09:25.431 --> 01:09:27.725
than as a drug with
some treatment effect.

01:09:27.726 --> 01:09:29.860
That actually boils down not to

01:09:29.861 --> 01:09:31.690
the individual fairness where

01:09:31.691 --> 01:09:33.095
you're assigning this metric on

01:09:33.096 --> 01:09:35.080
every pair of individuals in

01:09:35.081 --> 01:09:38.235
the population but only
like across groups.

01:09:38.236 --> 01:09:39.100
&gt;&gt; Right.

01:09:39.101 --> 01:09:42.340
&gt;&gt; Then you do allow meritocracy
within a group but you

01:09:42.341 --> 01:09:44.500
want placeboness across groups.

01:09:44.501 --> 01:09:45.430
It's good to address this.

01:09:45.431 --> 01:09:48.300
&gt;&gt; This is really interesting
and I don't know.

01:09:48.301 --> 01:09:54.420
I mentioned that in
the causality literature,

01:09:54.421 --> 01:09:57.215
there's been work sort
of in the Pearl model.

01:09:57.216 --> 01:10:00.120
In the Rubin model,
I don't know of any.

01:10:00.121 --> 01:10:01.410
Maybe there, but I
don't know of any,

01:10:01.411 --> 01:10:03.210
and I want to look at that.

01:10:03.211 --> 01:10:06.195
I think this is definitely
worth thinking through,

01:10:06.196 --> 01:10:11.755
so very good question.
Wait, someone, yes?

01:10:11.756 --> 01:10:15.700
&gt;&gt; I was wondering
about something.

01:10:15.701 --> 01:10:18.100
This title is about
this going towards

01:10:18.101 --> 01:10:20.760
the theory of modeling fairness.

01:10:20.761 --> 01:10:22.740
I was thinking, have people

01:10:22.741 --> 01:10:24.870
tried to turn it around
and say that you're

01:10:24.871 --> 01:10:26.570
going to accept that
things are going to be

01:10:26.571 --> 01:10:29.205
unfair and we want to actually

01:10:29.206 --> 01:10:34.240
quantify the degree of
how unfair things are.

01:10:34.241 --> 01:10:35.950
In other words, instead of

01:10:35.951 --> 01:10:37.995
treating them as
fairness constraints,

01:10:37.996 --> 01:10:41.535
try to put them into
maybe equivalence classes

01:10:41.536 --> 01:10:44.110
of the way people study

01:10:44.111 --> 01:10:46.840
program classes and say B and

01:10:46.841 --> 01:10:51.515
D. Do you have any thoughts
in that direction?

01:10:51.516 --> 01:10:54.005
&gt;&gt; I only know of

01:10:54.006 --> 01:10:57.950
one theoretical result
along those lines,

01:10:57.951 --> 01:11:01.190
which when I was talking about

01:11:01.191 --> 01:11:05.835
when individual fairness
implies statistical parody.

01:11:05.836 --> 01:11:09.560
In fact, there's a tighter
characterization of how

01:11:09.561 --> 01:11:11.620
unfair it is in terms of

01:11:11.621 --> 01:11:14.650
the move or distance
between groups.

01:11:15.180 --> 01:11:20.720
I don't know of, I guess,

01:11:20.721 --> 01:11:25.470
some of the very recent works
do look at what

01:11:25.471 --> 01:11:27.660
happens if you relax
things a little bit like

01:11:27.661 --> 01:11:30.915
that probably
approximately fair notion.

01:11:30.916 --> 01:11:32.310
You're saying, "Okay,
we'll introduce

01:11:32.311 --> 01:11:33.320
a little bit of slack,

01:11:33.321 --> 01:11:35.170
what can we get from it?"

01:11:35.171 --> 01:11:37.520
There is some of that,

01:11:37.521 --> 01:11:41.040
but I don't know of
wholesale saying, "Okay.

01:11:41.041 --> 01:11:43.470
Well, we're just going to
treat these people this way."

01:11:43.471 --> 01:11:45.200
I don't know.

01:11:45.201 --> 01:11:46.770
&gt;&gt; I was wondering, in
differential privacy,

01:11:46.771 --> 01:11:48.480
there's this trade
off of, let's say,

01:11:48.481 --> 01:11:50.120
the epsilon parameter you said to

01:11:50.121 --> 01:11:53.725
get somewhere on that curve.

01:11:53.726 --> 01:11:58.360
You give up privacy
to get more accuracy.

01:11:58.800 --> 01:12:01.490
&gt;&gt; Yes. If you think about it,

01:12:01.491 --> 01:12:03.180
the definition of
fairness is very

01:12:03.181 --> 01:12:05.400
similar in flavor to
the definition of

01:12:05.401 --> 01:12:07.720
differential privacy but it

01:12:07.721 --> 01:12:11.165
doesn't behave the same way
under composition.

01:12:11.166 --> 01:12:15.545
Yes. There is
one result also about

01:12:15.546 --> 01:12:18.420
using a particular algorithm

01:12:18.421 --> 01:12:22.430
from differential privacy
to ensure fairness,

01:12:23.720 --> 01:12:26.380
where the trade-off
that you talk about

01:12:26.381 --> 01:12:28.650
would perhaps be relevant.

01:12:28.651 --> 01:12:34.430
Yes. Anyone else?

01:12:34.431 --> 01:12:37.300
Yes.

01:12:37.301 --> 01:12:40.730
&gt;&gt; I heard a question
about the practice.

01:12:41.310 --> 01:12:44.305
This problem is complicated.

01:12:44.306 --> 01:12:46.990
